> > Absent that differentiatior, Alpha has no value proposition.
>
> I don't believe that Alpha's performance lead was not sustainable
> over IA64. I think it is more likely that the size of the performance
> "headroom" that IA64 would leave on the demand side of the market was
> too small.
>
> That is, the number of customers not satisfied with performance
> achievable with standard IA64 products and willing to pay for higher
> performance, low volume Alpha hardware would not be large enough to
> justify the high costs of staying ahead of IA64.

That's an awfully general assertion that requires detailed examination.

Given that EV8's costs are already largely paid and that IA64 shows no signs
of being performance-competitive within EV8's lifetime (IA64 likely wouldn't
even qualify as a mature, dependable platform in mid-range or higher server
configurations on EV8's release), the ability to justify completing EV8 on
the basis of the additional short-/medium-term profit it would generate
seems clear (especially when compared to the hit Alpha products seem certain
to take over that time period with the current decision) - and that would
have given IA64 an opportunity to offer considerably more proof of its
ability to compete beyond that point in time (if indeed it proves able to do
so).

The fact that IA64 has absolutely no guaranteed market (i.e., no large
market it shows any sign of being the highest-performance, let alone best
cost/performance, entry in for the foreseeable future - since its costs will
only come down after demand has grown, and no obvious source of high-volume
demand exists) makes assumptions about its future (and particularly about
the volumes of it and of high-end servers based on it) murky at best (see
iAPX432 for a historical example).  If Intel does succeed in muscling it
through, it seems likely to be for reasons having little to do with IA64's
innate ability to compete (and Alpha's lack of same).

- bill





