I have very fond memories of the DEC-20 and TOPS-20 since those were the
first "real big" machines I used (started on apples/commodores, eventually
got an account on the local university's DEC-20).  It was really funny moving
from the world of having a small PC in front of me with color graphics and sound
(as limited as the Apple IIe's color and sound were ;-) ) to a black and
white terminal.  But I did like the idea that many people were using the same
machine and could communicate with each other using mail and messaging.
I didn't have nearly as much fun when the uni "upgraded" to VAX/VMS and I
started using that.  While the help system was quite good (I learned pretty
much all of DCL and the commands from reading it) I found DCL quite annoying,
and when I was developing C software (I wrote a MUD client called 'DINK',
based on 'TINT') hated the arcane syntax needed to link dynamically (with
a disk quota of only 250 blocks, it was hard to link my app statically
against the C runtime).  Further, I disliked the [DRIVENAME]:filename setup.
As soon as I could, I escaped to unix (telnet across the country to an account
a friend set up at another university) and a few years later discovered
