Sander Vesik <sander@haldjas.folklore.ee> writes:

> 
> In comp.arch Dave Hansen <iddw@hotmail.com> wrote:
> > On 18 Jul 2001 21:11:14 -0600, Joe Pfeiffer <pfeiffer@cs.nmsu.edu>
> > wrote:
> 
> > [...]
> >>``int'' isn't longest integral type, ``long int'' is (long long int
> >>never made it into ANSI, did it?  If it did, I'll stand corrected).
> 
> > You stand corrected.  In C99, "long long" is guaranteed to be a 64-bit
> > type.  The new longest int is intmax_t.
> 
> Is it really *guranteed* to be 64 bits? So what do you call a 128bit type
> made up of two 64 bit halves? What if you do have 128 bit types in hardware?

The number of bugs caused by programmers "knowing" that C defines char, short,
int, long (and now, God forbid, that long long atrocity) to be 8, 16, 32, and
32 bits long is mind-boggling.  For as far back as I can recall, the only
guarantee that C made was that a long was not shorter than a short.  When I was
a KSR-1 kernel developer, we had a compiler for which short was 16, int 32, and
long 64, and a pointer was 40 bits.  The amount of Unix code that assumed that
sizeof(int) == sizeof(long) and sizeof(<ptr>) == sizeof(int) was mind-boggling
-- we spent months (maybe even years) stomping out bugs caused by this
brain-damage.  I recall we finally moved the kernel's address space base to
0xf800000000 to more quickly catch all the bugs that assumed that pointers and
ints were the same size.


