You can boot the Tru64 UNIX CD-ROM from which the system was installed from,
assuming you have the kit at hand. Once the OS is running, you can drop out
of the installation program to single user mode and utilize the software to
repair your disk drives partitions or file structures.

Without a good look at how the original system was installed, and a very
detailed look at the configuration of the system and a strong backgound in
UNIX system management, then the likelyhood of success is low to moderate.

I am seeing more and more of these cries from the woods for help these days,
and finding that so-called "management", made a choice on what to buy, or
how to finance or to drop the maintenance contract or what have you, and in
all of these cases, I am told that there is no money available, not for
repairs, not for training and not to hire someone with the requisite
knowledege to make it work again. That being the case, I am highlighting the
fuitle posiiton that some people find themselves in, confronted by their
"managers" to pick up the pieces now that the former knowledge people have
been downsized out of the business and the remaing staff are confronted with
complex problems for which they have no adequate training to enable them to
deal with them.

I put it to you, your so-called management created this problem, are
pounding on you to resolve it, and are not going to provide you with the
proper means to do so. So you turn to the internet, where some kind souls
offer up some suggestions to you, you go back and something sort of works or
even does work, and everyone in your place of business concludes that
management was right, you don't need knowledgeable workers on staff, and you
have done the rest of us un-employed knowledge workers a dis-service and set
yourself up for failure on the next problem cause it's going to be even more
difficult to resolve.

There used to be some good advice kicking around, something like, "Don't go
to the mechanic with a toothache." Sure the mechanic is cheaper, and he can
probably use his pliers to pull the aching tooth out of your head, but he's
not part of a properly managed health plan, is he? Well, a 20 year old
hacker that works for $18.00 an hour is a poor substitute for a properly run
Information Technology department, whihc is where many businesses seem to be
going these days.

