In article <3B469F39.618E0ADE@igs.net>, Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> writes:
|> 
|> 
|> mulp wrote:
...
|> > 
|> > The reason for not implementing byte/word ops was to avoid having to do a
|> > read/modify/write operation to update memory.  To write a byte into memory,
|> > you must read the longword containing the byte, do any required ECC
|> > corrections, insert the byte, compute a new ECC, and then update memory.
|> 
|> 
|> Nice try but no. The original Alpha ISA left out 8 and 16 bit loads
|> and stores to simplify the load aligner shift network between the
|> data cache and the CPU registers/bypass paths, a common critical
|> path.

I was a member of the original Alpha Architecture design team (then called
the RISCy VAX design team).  Mulp correctly describes the reason why Alpha
originally did not have 8- and 16-bit loads/stores.  It was believed that
future implementations would have write-back level 1 caches and no competent
hardware implementation engineer would do a write-back without ECC checking.
There were strong arguments in favor of not supporting 32-bit loads/stores in
order to make ECC checking/writing even easier.  But RISCy VAX's original
design charter was to be a top performing scientific machine and 32-bit floats
are very important to some Fortran applications (like signal processing).  It
was ironic that by the time RISCy VAX had its name changed to Alpha and started
shipping that VMS (the first OS that ran on Alpha) was now more important as
a commercial/enterprise operating system rather than a scientific/Fortran OS.
Also, no Alpha implementation ever used a write-back level 1 cache.  It turned
out that write-buffers was a better implementation technique for improving the
performance of store operations.


