The 5100 is native PostScript Level 2.

For the printer to print PCL, it has to load and run a PostScript program
that is a PCL interpreter, as in,

The PostScript engine runs the PCL program. Data to be printed comes in on
one of the ports. The data is passed on to the PCL PostScript porgram, which
must then output PostScript data to go to the PostScript engine, which can
then start imaging a page. Yes it can be slower. The frimware change I
emtnioned was to prevent hangs when major error events occurred. Initially,
these were only correctable by power cycling.

As for the disk drive, used to be a standard feature on many good PostScript
printers, it provided local caching storage of fonts, thus dramatically
speeding printing by reducing the download effort.

