The 5305 system is a former AlphaServer 1200, that has a unique design of
the CPU module. The SROM chip is in fact socketed on this module and is used
strictly for POST. An Extended SROM was incorporated into this system, which
in fact is located in Flash EEPROM. As such, if you were to load the AS1200
firmware update against this machine, you'd end up with a an AlphaServer
1200, capable of loading and running OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX.

There is an environment variable that should be set to tell the OS what kind
of machine it is.

One of the worst problems ever with this machine was the abysmally
manufactured metal work, in and around the floppy disk drive. If all four
screws are used to mount the floppy disk drive, this will stretch the floppy
disk drive frame out of alignment and cause read/write failures on the inner
tracks.
After battling the sustaining engineering group for 2 months on this
problem, the enforced solution was to mount the floppy disk drive with only
1 screw. Thus, if you experience problem with your floppy, remove all but
one screw.

The 3300 machine I am not familiar with, it was probably similar to a
workstation and may in fact have an XSROM like the AS1200.

rtt



