I figured this out many months ago when I installed VMS on my M48...we'll
see how much I recall correctly now. :-)

The first number (01400000) is the memory size.  It's a hexadecimal
number, and IIRC is in bytes.

If you removed  the memory board you should have 4 Megs on the system
board, which should show up as 400000 (hex)

01400000 is 20 Megs, so it looks like you have 16 Megs on the memory
board(s)..

You can install two memory boards.  One connects to the system board, the
other is a smaller daughter board that connects to the main memory board
and is installed below it.

The M38 (and M48) take a maximum of 32 Megs, 4 Megs on the system board,
16 Megs on the main memory board and 12 Megs on the daughter memory board.

I think you can get 4, 8, 12 and 16 Meg memory board and 4, 8 and 12 Meg
daughter boards.  I had a couple of different memory and daughter boards
and all combinations seem to work.

I gave up trying to look up part numbers, I just used the console and
tested each memory board without any daughter boards and then with a
daughter board to figure out what I had.

I have a couple of Dataram boards, they work fine.

>assuming no ECC.
Correct, no ECC in the M38.

>My next stop is the SCSI CDROM and hobbiest CD collections.

