> For the printer to print PCL, it has to load and run a PostScript program
> that is a PCL interpreter, as in,
> 
> The PostScript engine runs the PCL program. Data to be printed comes in on
> one of the ports. The data is passed on to the PCL PostScript porgram, which
> must then output PostScript data to go to the PostScript engine, which can
> then start imaging a page.

PostScript and PCL are two separate interpreters in this, and I believe
most, printers.  PCL is not interpreted under PostScript.  And data
passed to the print engine is an image, not in any particular PDL like
PostScript or PCL.

The PCL in the DEClaser 5100 may be slow, but it's not due to the
reason you mention.  Printer characteristics such as performance vary
more because of the controller, memory and other electronics specific
to that model rather than the printer engine used.

