Richard Tomkins wrote:

> Please demonstrate or elaborate your claims.

Well if I had an NT-only Alpha I'd have a go :-)
AFAIK, someone somewhere managed to convinve 
themselves that NT-only Alphas were a good idea.
Mainly (I suppose) so they could sell them
(relatively) cheaply without having to
drop the margins on OpenVMS and Tru64.
The easiest way to make these machines
unable to boot other OSes was to mangle
the hardware ID in the HWRPB. Since
that is a known location you can potentially
unmangle it with the appropriate SRM
console command. 

> As a manufacturing engineer involved in the building of many of these former
> products, someone who had been in intimate contact with the design
> engineering community within DIGITAL and later COMPAQ, I ma fairly certain
> of the information provided to me by the engineering community at large.

I watched a Digital Semi engineer do this
once to (IIRC) one of the early -a series
boxes. If memory serves correctly, for
some unfathomable reason the internal
cost of the -a boxes was less than 
the -au boxes. So they ordered the cheapest
box and fiddled.

So I know that *some* boxes that were
NT-only *can* run OpenVMS and Tru64.
I never had to do that myself so I have
no notes on exactly which machines
one could reasonably expect to do this
with and only the merest hints in my notebook
about exactly what to do.

BTW: although the SRM sources were (and
probably still are) tightly controlled,
the SROM was much less so. In fact there
were sources for some machines available
in the DS EBSDK. So in principle, you 
could make your own SROM that set the
ID any way you want or use it to make
sense of a disassembly of the existing SROM
(always assuming that the machine you are
playing with is near enough to one that
was supported by the EBSDK).

Antonio

