> supporters (IBM PC group and Compaq) and Compaq/Sequent due to the poor NUMA
> design of their systems.
> 
> >On the other hand, if TPC-C is
> >just another meaningless benchmark number - then lets stop talking about it
> >altogether - or define a meaningful test.
> >
> Actually, for single instance, TPC-C is one of the best benchmarks
> out there.  It stresses the system greatly for a single database
> instance.  Its only problem is that vendors took advantage of the
> partitionability of the workload to generate huge numbers when they
> could not do so with a single box.  This made it appear they were in
> the game when they really were not.   The TPC fixed this somewhat
> by creating a "clustered" list of results and "non-clustered" list
> of results.  Unfortunately, the GS320 number is considered "non-clustered"
> because the OS is single instance even though the database is not.
> 
> So, the real problem is the misclassification of the GS320 (and Sequent
> numbers previously) as non-cluster results when in fact they used a
> clustered database.
> 
> --Chris
> My own views.

I fully agree. Let me add a bit to this, perhaps explaining more. 

In what's below, RAC = Oracle's version of their cluster database
(multiple Oracle instances) that has been (re-)designed to run
under a single OS instance with better management.

As said, TPC-C is based on a warehouse model; it has order
transactions for parts, payment transactions, etc. You can have
multiple warehouses, and when you do, it is built into the
benchmark that separate warehouses don't communicate much. By
measurement, only 7.5% of the data accesses are cross-warehouse.
(You have to dig into the benchmark's spec of the random number
generator to find this out.)

So, with RAC (or a cluster), you just arrange things so that no
warehouse is split among the instances of Oracle running on the
box. Result: There's so little communication, you may as well be
running N copies of the benchmark at the same time, and adding up
what each does to get the TPC-C tpmc rating. 

It scales like crazy; you can get 85% to 90% efficiency on a
cluster of, for example, 16 separate nodes connected by 10Mbit
Ethernet. For all practical purposes, the total TPC-C rating is
limited only by how many systems the vendors can afford to
assemble in one place and can keep working for the benchmark
duration (often a limit due to disk failures; *lots* of disks
needed).

There is *nothing* illegal about doing this; it's part of the
benchmark definition. Every company publishing a "cluster" TPC-C
result uses this. In fact, I do know that every last
not-in-a-box, on a cluster, OPS benchmark that's been published
has used this partitioning trick, and I also know that some
pre-RAC cases of OPS used in a single system have used it. The
same is true for other databases than Oracle.

Nevertheless, very few customers have workloads that divide that
precisely and evenly with so little communication.

Note that use of RAC alone is not *proof* that this technique was
in fact used. RAC (or any use of OPS) makes doing this trick
convenient. It's harder with a single instance; I don't know any
case were it was used with a single database instance. But use of
RAC doesn't prove it was used. They could have distributed all
the warehouses uniformly across the Oracle instances. Since that
would give lower performance numbers, I tend to doubt that they
did (as you said, it's the benchmark game), but it is
theoretically possible.

That's why I asked if anybody knew for a fact that this technique
was used. I've since had offline feedback that it in fact was
used.

Greg Pfister
<not necessarily my employer's opinion>


