You can't compare an Athlon's ASP with an Alpha's recurring development cost
(not to speak of the non-recurring development cost). While the business
models of Alpha and Athlon are different, the manufacturing costs alone and
especially the fixed part of those costs (cf. DEC and the Hudson fab) can
be a significant issue.

> And, as I've observed elsewhere, at current run rates the IA64 per-processor
> development burden likely approaches the GNP of many smaller countries.

Again, this is not comparable. Before the first revenue chip/system is
shipped, the development burden per processor is infinite. Nonetheless,
it may make economic sense to develop a new architecture, because you
expect to spread the cost of development (both non-recurring and recurring)
over a sufficient number of revenue shipment over the years. All this tells
you at the moment about IA64 is that Intel must sell (for a development cost
of $10^10 as an order-of-magnitude estimate) on the order of 10^7-8 processors
in the next few years to make economic sense. Whether that goal is achievable
is another matter.

	Jan


