Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) writes:

> > Finally, I believe that the OS/360 people used the term "single 
> > image" to mean multiple systems in a cluster booting off the same
> > system disk, so that they would be running the same system version
> > everywhere. VMS cluster *can* do this, but it is not required.
> > In fact, a VMS cluster could theoretically run on nodes with dis-
> > similar architectures; certainly with different system versions.
> I nominate "single system image" for an obscured computing-terminology
> award that I guess we will have to call the "Open" award.

I do think that, while the name given to this feature is misleading, the
feature has merit. If you are booting a VMS cluster off a common system 
root in VMS's terminology, you _know_ that the versions of a lodable modules
and other code making up the kernel on the various systems are the same (if
no installation screw-ups have occured). Thus, you know that any differences
in behaviour you might be seeing are not caused by a different version of one
of the hundreds of files making up the kernel being loaded on one machine and
not the other. And it gives you the ability to upgrade etc. all machines in
one go, should you so desire. (Of course, VMS also offers, as Larry says, the
ability to run on more complex set-ups, including different hardware
architectures and/or versions of the kernel simultaneously.) This removes a
headache I have often seen on various systems.

	Jan 


