In article <3B57A5E7.26C2B49E@jetnet.ab.ca>, 
bfranchuk@jetnet.ab.ca says...
> > So, those who need an 128-bit type on a 64-bit platform are out of luck,
> > at least for the time being.  Although int128_t would be the solution for
> > their needs, I'm not aware of any implementation providing it.
> 
> The thing is you have to have a type 2x the longest word size in some
> form to handle multiply and divide components. It will show up but it is not
> something that marketing brags about.

That's not even true in hardware. And certainly not in 
C or C++. In C/C++ there is a largest size integral 
value represented by the compiler -- usually long or 
long long. Those values can be multiplied and divided, 
but there is not even internal support for a value 
twice that size. Not only that, but for C++ where 
there is a long long type, multiplying two longs may 
result in a long instead of a long long because long 
long is not yet part of the C++ standard and widening 
the result would violate the existing standard. You 
have to explicitly widen the operands before you 
multiply.

On the hardware side, there are machines which have 
multiply instructions that provide only the bottom 
half or the top half of the double precision result. I 
see nothing fundamental, even in hardware, that 
requires the top half of the result ever be available. 
Likewise, the hardware might do a divide by internally 
widening the result, dividing and then providing the 
quotient and remainder -- all without ever exposing a 
double precision value.


