Last week, both cache batteries of our pair of HSJ40 died (two weeks after
the service contract ended) and the Mirror- and RAID-Sets got "INOPERATIVE"
and my (CI) VMScluster freezed.

I looked into the HSJ, saw the situation and rebooted the HSJ for a quick
start and the cluster continued. And then the weekend (with a visit by my
newborn nieces ;-) came.

A couple of hours later the HSJ repeated the hang. Appearantly the
cache batteries got "low" on reboot and this couple of hours later
the HSJ declared the cache battery as "failed" (note: the CACHE is GOOD,
only the Cache Battery is FAILED) and therefore the disks got "INOPERATIVE"
again.

So, I learned that I had the wrong CACHE_POLICY set (A, instead of B) and
fixed it, rebooted the HSJ and the cluster continued. But a couple of hours
later, the hang returned.

So, I learned to switch off WRITEBACK_CACHE of all mirror/raid sets
(Is this correct ?) Rebooted the HSJs and the cluster continued.


This morning, the cluster was freezed again.
Seems, I don't understand why a good cache battery is required to run disks
on the HSJ in READ_CACHE only mode.

Currently, I have a HSJ which has a 'FAILED' cache battery just from the
startup, which does not serve disks regardless the PREFERRED_PATH in the HSJ
and a HSJ which has a 'LOW' cache battery from boot on, serving all disks
until the HSJ declares the cache battery 'FAILED' a couple of hours later.

What to do now ? I can live with reduced functionality/performance,
but I can't live with cluster hangs twice a day...

Do I have to replace the batteries at all (which is hard without a contract) ?
Do I have to dismount the batteries to get a working cache again
	(or is there some parameter) ?
Do I have to disable the READ_CACHE on all device units, too (unlikely) ?
Do I have to reboot the whole VMScluster (unlikely) ?

Again, do I really need HSJ cache batteries to use MIRROR/RAID sets ?

Many TIA

