> performing single node/single instance system.

It's the database that's clustered, not Wildfire.

Wildfire is NOT a clustered system (at least not by my definition). 
It is a single system running a single coherence protocol and a single
OS with a single system image.  It is a ccNUMA system, which by it's
very nature places some of the burden on the programmer to maximize
performance.  Maybe the programs are not yet sophisticated to the
point where the messiness is transparent to the user or database
admin, but it's just a matter of time before they are.

> 2. Pretend that they have non clustered system when in fact 
> they have a single system image but with multiple instances 
> because without multiple instances the system does not 
> perform.

That's argumentative.  1) You are assuming that Wildfire's performance
would be pathetic without running the clustered database
instances--which is an assumption based on facts not in evidence.  2)
You are assuming that people who buy $10 million systems don't know
the difference between a single instance database and a multiple
instance database.  3) You are assuming that there are not customers
who will run the multiple instance versions to get the maximum
performance.

> 3. Compare favourably this system with an equally performant 
> system which used one system image and one DBMS instance.

Wildfire is a single system image!  Say that three times.

If a customer is truley limited to a single DBMS instance, then they
should ask their vendors to produce benchmarks accordingly before they
purchase.  If it is true that everybody is limited this way (which I
doubt), then the rules of TPC-C should be changed.

> The fact that your employer has done all three of these things 
> tends to inject a note of unintentional irony into your
> "just another meaningless benchmark number" comment. Compaq
> has hardly done TPC-C any favours in this respect.

Look, I'm not a huge Wildfire fan or a database expert, but I do know
that there are no 'good' companies or 'bad' companies when it comes to
benchmarking.  You lay down the rules of the contest and you let
companies compete.  If the benchmark restrictions are not
representative of every customer's workload, then that is the fault of
the benchmark.

As it stands, the Wildfire numbers are valid under the rules of
submission for TPC-C.

Don't pretend that Sun or HP (or Intel) are any more moral than Compaq
when it comes to benchmarking.  I would love for you to try and argue
this point.

