>Hi Bill,
>
>Interesting comments you made about Compaq financials.  They can
>sometimes be a goldmine of information. You are quite right about the PC
>market (personal and commercial) being a consistent money loser and
>dragging Compaq down.
>
>A couple of brief comments:
>
>- In a GOOD quarter PC's seem to return a profit about 1 or 2% of the
>money spent on them; in most quarters they lose money and have done for
>the last few years.  By contrast Dell are returning about 6-10%. 
>Enterprise stuff from Compaq regularly returns about 14%.
>
>- In the Annual Report for 2000 (ie. December) Compaq says about
>Commercial Personal Computing, "Costs for processors, memory and hard
>drives for desktops and portables declined during the year.", and about
>Consumer PC's "Higher component costs also contributed to lower
>operating income."  The contradiction is not impossible but it does
>deserve an explanation.
>
What this probably means (from someone who has had to decipher this
stuff on and off for 20 years or so) is that the first "cost" is the amount 
they spent overall, while the second is the cost per unit. Which would
imply that volume purchased (and shipped) declined substantially.

There seems to be a Gresham's law in the computer biz: when floundering,
cannibalize your profitable prospects to save the unprofitable ones (which
of course makes sense, because no one would pay you to buy one large
money-losing commodity PC business...)

paul


