Correct.  The database is "clustered" and this is where the issue
arises.  Unfortunately, I believe the multiple Oracle instances might
be considered as "partitions" by TPC-C definitions given the TPC-C
probably was not thinking about people running a clustered database
on a single instance.   In fact, the "cluster" area in the results was
just recently done and no where in the executive summary do you have
do disclose that a "cluster" was used.

> It is a ccNUMA system, which by it's
>very nature places some of the burden on the programmer to maximize
>performance.  Maybe the programs are not yet sophisticated to the
>point where the messiness is transparent to the user or database
>admin, but it's just a matter of time before they are.
>
Only if the ccNUMA has bad memory characteristics as the GS320 does.
HP's Superdome is ccNUMA as well (some memory is accessed at different
latencies than others), but it does not have problems running
a single instance of the database.  Customers running on a Superdome
don't have to partition their data or worry about NUMA issues.  Only
the GS320 seems to need to worry about this.  I believe SGI's first
NUMA boxes had the same problem as the GS320, but current ones
don't (from what I've heard).  Given SGI doesn't run commercial tests
on the Origin, it's hard to really tell.

