>> they have a single system image but with multiple instances 
>> because without multiple instances the system does not 
>> perform.
>
>That's argumentative.  1) You are assuming that Wildfire's performance
>would be pathetic without running the clustered database
>instances--which is an assumption based on facts not in evidence.  
>
There was evidence, but Compaq withdrew the number.  Compaq did a
single instance GS320 number that was really bad and shortly followed
it up (I believe within a month) with an OPS number that was 18%
better.  They then followed that up with another number later that
was 27% better (same OS, database, processors), so I assume they
found more ways to make the ccNUMA look OK.

>You are assuming that people who buy $10 million systems don't know
>the difference between a single instance database and a multiple
>instance database.  3) You are assuming that there are not customers
>who will run the multiple instance versions to get the maximum
>performance.
>
I'm sure there are some customers willing to do this, but not many.
IT costs are too high to be doing dumb stuff to get around performance
problems when the customer could just buy a box that performs better
without having all of the headaches.

--Chris
