Uh, no.  It means nothing of the sort, as anyone whose thought
about what goes into a PC BOM (bill-of-materials) already knows.
Go wander around any computer retailer and look around first.
Then, and I'm not speaking for Intel BTW, consider:

Sure, the components called out (processors, memory and
hard drives) have declined in cost.  But quite a few things
have not.  Let's think about it a bit, shall we:

Sheet metal : yeah, the basic case would probably cost the same,
since metal gauges haven't quite shrunk as fast as chip geometries.
Design considerations, however, have led to more stylish
(and therefor more expensive) cases in the mainstream.

Cooling solutions: hey, that processor sure is cheap, but at
70W power dissipation, you need a bigger heatsink and/or
a more powerful fan.

Power delivery: all that heat came from somewhere, and that
somewhere is the case power supply and the on-motherboard
voltage regulators.  Plus there's all those nifty new sleep-mode
power requirements.  Probably a cost increase there as well.

Additional I/O interfaces: USB and Firewire ports aren't free,
but you didn't used to need them, or at least, so many of them.
And unfortunately most of the market

More sophisticated I/O devices: DVD-ROM and CD-RW drives
are all over the place: both cost more than a CD-ROM drive. But
people want them; however, they won't pay much more  for them.

Now come on: isn't it obvious that processor, memory and hard drive
aren't the only components in a PC ? Didn't that occur to you ?

Feh: foolish naive amateurs, pretending to have clues they don't ...
and these people have the gall to denigrate Compaq's decisions ?
They don't even know the _basics_.
