Sun Micro President Upbeat About Co.'s Server Chip

NEW YORK -- Compaq Computer Corp.'s (CPQ) decision to stop making Alpha
chips has been boon for Sun Microsystems Inc. (SUNW).

The Palo Alto, Calif., computer maker has been running ads targeting Alpha
customers and is receiving thousands of inquiries on its Web site about its
UltraSparc III chip, said Sun President and Chief Operating Officer Edward
Zander.

Last month, Compaq said it will phase out its Alpha chip used in its servers
and replace it with Intel Corp.'s (INTC) Itanium microprocessor by 2004.
Compaq will make one more version of Alpha. Compaq said when it announced
its plans that it talked with customers and is confident sales won't suffer
during the transition.

But Alpha customers don't know what to do, Zander said. "They have to wait
two years (for the Itanium)," he said.

He noted that Compaq's strategy will increase Sun's sales of the UltraSparc
III, although he declined to provide specifics.






