Fred Kleinsorge wrote:
> 
> Then explain to me why TPC-C numbers have any meaning at all.  Or why Oracle
> would waste good money developing such a thing - if nobody uses it.

I didn't know that the Transaction Performance Council was an Oracle
front :-> 

Think of TPC benchmarks as "spec benchmarks for databases".
Unfortunately,
the relative tuning/configuration space for a database/computer system
is very large, which leaves a lot of latitude in massaging the numbers.
Also, a benchmark is by its very nature is a static target, while
customer applications evolve over time.

As for meaning... TPC-C started out IIRC as a replacement for TPC-A
and TPC-B, earlier transaction benchmarks. However, the transactions
in these were very "lightweight", and not very representative of
"real world" DB transactions.

So TPC-C was created as a more representative benchmark.  Unfortunately,
like any benchmark, it has a finite lifetime, as benchmarkers look
for ways to make their systems look good, sometimes even if that
makes their benchmark configuration totally unrealistic for customer
purposes (e.g., non-RAID disk, turn off the redo logs (!), using
the "cluster cheat", etc.) You really have to dig into the complete
descriptions to find out what the vendor actually did, and then judge
if that makes the result a "benchmark special".

A similar thing happened to TPC-D, the data warehousing benchmark.
The early numbers probably correlated fairly well to DW system
performance, but as time went on, "hacks" (as well as legitimate
performance improvements) eventually made the exercise useless for
customers. In particular, some of the "ad hoc" queries (which were
intended to require significant database sweeps/scans) were turned
into order(1) table lookups by precomputing all the necessary indexing
information when the database was loaded. Benchmark legal,
but now it's not really an "ad hoc" query anymore (you can't reasonably
precompute all possible combinations of indices), making the
benchmark numbers useless for customers.

TPC-D is now retired, (with at least one company submitting a
"benchmark special" (as above) result just hours before the window
closed to claim bragging rights - sigh); I'd expect
TPC-C will follow at some point.


> 
