"Fred Kleinsorge" <kleinsorge@star.zko.dec.com> wrote in message
news:U1%47.872$rc5.61019@news.cpqcorp.net...

...

> OK, OK, you knew what I meant...  Why would Oracle spend good money on
OPS.

1.  OPS was developed over a decade ago, when available SMP systems had far
less headroom than they have today.

2.  But even today it has value (just as clusters still have value even
though large SMP systems can handle *most* major workloads) in, e.g.,
availability and additional scalability.

The problem with using cluster configurations with TPC-C is that TPC-C
operation is so readily partitionable that you can get just about any number
you want (just keep adding hardware).  Most real-world database use is more
intertwined (or at least sufficiently hard to partition effectively that
most people don't), such that you can't just keep adding cluster members and
scale performance anything like linearly.

So while TPC-C running on non-clustered configurations may give you at least
*some* idea of the relative performance of different platforms, TPC-C
running clustered is fairly useless (at least unless at a minimum you
compare configurations with equal numbers of cluster nodes, and even then
other particulars of the test configuration can make significant
differences).

- bill





