In article <seY47.836$rc5.60878@news.cpqcorp.net>,
Fred Kleinsorge <kleinsorge@star.zko.dec.com> wrote:
>OK.  I'm, not a database expert, but why is this a bad thing?  If TPC-C
>truly measures relative performance that could be expected in the real
>world - and if partitioning the system gets you this performance - then
>what's the big deal?  Tru64 clusters do provide a single system image,
>making it relatively easy to administer.  
>
Yes, the system might be easy to administer (although all of the flags you
need to set to get things like replicated text working might be more of
a hassle than for a non-NUMA box) but the database side is not given
you need to setup partitions and have multiple instances.  It is much
harder to maintain the database than with a single instance. 

Also, most workloads are not as partitionable as TPC-C.  In order to
scale to larger and larger workloads, TPC-C had to almost be designed
to be easily partitionable.  It needed to scale up the workload as 
performance increased.  The easiest way to do that was to scale everything
by "warehouses."  Once you do this, however, it becomes trivial to 
partition the entire setup based on "warehouse."  This could have been
busted with a lot of cross warehouse communication/queries but
the amount of this type of traffic in TPC-C is extremely small. 

The only companies that use the TPC-C "cluster" hack are those that
don't have a machine big enough or fast enought to compete in the single
 instance area (where customers care).  This has been mainly Microsoft 
supporters (IBM PC group and Compaq) and Compaq/Sequent due to the poor NUMA 
design of their systems. 

>On the other hand, if TPC-C is
>just another meaningless benchmark number - then lets stop talking about it
>altogether - or define a meaningful test.
>
Actually, for single instance, TPC-C is one of the best benchmarks
out there.  It stresses the system greatly for a single database 
instance.  Its only problem is that vendors took advantage of the
partitionability of the workload to generate huge numbers when they
could not do so with a single box.  This made it appear they were in
the game when they really were not.   The TPC fixed this somewhat
by creating a "clustered" list of results and "non-clustered" list
of results.  Unfortunately, the GS320 number is considered "non-clustered"
because the OS is single instance even though the database is not.

So, the real problem is the misclassification of the GS320 (and Sequent
numbers previously) as non-cluster results when in fact they used a
clustered database.

--Chris
My own views.
