In article <UUZ47.856$rc5.60905@news.cpqcorp.net>,
Fred Kleinsorge <kleinsorge@star.zko.dec.com> wrote:
>Then explain to me why TPC-C numbers have any meaning at all.  Or why Oracle
>would waste good money developing such a thing - if nobody uses it.
>
TPC-C numbers are very meaningful for single instance databases
and OSes.  Whoever has the fastest single instance number will have
the best "cluster" number with enough money.  Right now, I'd claim
the leader for single instance is Fujitsu followed closely by IBM
and HP.  Compaq "appears" to be the leader given the GS320 number at 
1GHZ, but that was a "cluster in a can" and does not really count.
Sun and Compaq bring up the rear.

People do use Oracle's OPS, but they use it for reliability not
performance.  Typically the other "node" is just a hot standby
incase the main system goes down.  This allows the downtime to 
be minimal and is absolutely needed technology to run a 24x7 shop.

Notice that there are ZERO SAP OPS numbers.  There is a reason for
this.  The SAP benchmark does not easily partition, thus it does
not run well with OPS.  In this sense SAP actually represents 
real life better than TPC-C.  However, there are other issues that
make SAP benchmarks probably less reliable of a performance
predictor than TPC-C.

--Chris
