Greg Pfister wrote:
> >  Is VMS a complete kernel-level single system image?

"David J. Dachtera" wrote:
> No. Current VMS is a modular kernel. The concept of a system "image"
> does not exist.

I think there is a bit of terminology confusion here. In VMS, the
word "image" is used for what IBM mainframe people call a "load 
module", i.e. an executable file, output from the linker.
The VMS kernel is lined in several pieces, so that the initial
system image loads additional images in a modular fashion as the
system comes up, like Windows DLLs or OS/360 link pack modules.

In some operating systems, the term "single image" is used to mean
that a single copy of the operating system is loaded into shared
memory, and all processors execute the same copy of the operating
system. VMS *can* do this for SMP configurations, but normally
a cluster consists of nodes that do not share any memory but run
independently, with internodal communication via a fast LAN to
support a distributed lock manager.

Finally, I believe that the OS/360 people used the term "single 
image" to mean multiple systems in a cluster booting off the same
system disk, so that they would be running the same system version
everywhere. VMS cluster *can* do this, but it is not required.
In fact, a VMS cluster could theoretically run on nodes with dis-
similar architectures; certainly with different system versions.
