> does everything right and couldn't possibly be wrong in anything.

It's certainly true that some string functions in the C library --
notably printf, as you pointed out -- do a test for NULL and don't
break.  But as the other poster pointed out, the pointer is still
invalid, and there are other string functions that assume the
programmer knows what he or she is doing (like I didn't when I ran
into this, lo these many years ago), and the program will crash.

I have absolutely no idea what your comment about assuming Linux does
everything right could be referring to; when I ran into the problem,
it was porting from 4.2BSD on a VAX (where the bottom page is
readable, and coincidentally address 0 always contained a 0 byte), to
a reasonably early SunOS on a 680x0 (where the bottom page isn't
readable).
