Joe Pfeiffer <pfeiffer@cs.nmsu.edu> writes:

>Real fun was porting VAX to Sun.  I honestly thought C specified that
>a NULL pointer was an empty string.  Passing a pointer to an int with
>a value of less than 256 to a function expecting a pointer to a char
>was a good one, too.


That's probably why HP-UX mapped a page of 0s at 0x0, so dereferencing
a NULL pointer wouldn't cause a segmentation fault.  There is an option
to disable this mapping when you compile the executable (it is mapped in
by the loader) but last time I checked (a few years ago) it was still
the default.  That's the sort of ugly thing the market makes you do
because if you don't, people will say "this machine sucks, it can't run
this perfectly good C code, which I know is good because it runs on that
other machine over there just fine"  Once it is in there, making it no
longer the default would probably break enough old software that a lot
of people would stupidly assume the new version of the compiler is
buggy.

I'm sure HP isn't the only one who did this sort of ugly thing.  It was
kind of nice how Alpha left the whole 32 bit chunk at 0x0 unmapped to
make it more easy to catch int<->pointer problems.  Though I'm still a
bit unhappy about their decision to have a 32 bit int in the first place,
now we are stuck with that decision forever in 64 bit Unix, and the
original meaning of "int" (largest integral type) is no longer valid.

--
