That is exactly what I meant by "all other things being equal".
Not all programmers are equal.

>>There are standard methods of counting lines of code, and tools
>>that do it.  When comparing programs, it does not really matter
>>what your rules are for "lines of code", it only matters that
>>you use the same rules for all programs.
> 
> And this works?  Or is it one of those metrics that look
> impressive in upper level management book reports.

It is a somewhat useful prediction of the number of defects for
which one should search.  If the number of defects found is low
by 50% or high by 100% I would consider that reasonable.  If it
is low by an order of magnitude (compared to similar programs
created in a similar fashion), the testing job is suspect.  If
the defect count is high by an order of magnitude, this program
is worse than your average program.

Of course the defect counts above refer to the count after
one has reached a significant decrease in the number of defects
found per unit of effort spent finding them.


