Yes, all of the GS320 TPC-C numbers have been a cluster in a can
with OPS except the very first one they published and withdrew (because
it was so poor in terms of performance).  If you look at the full disclosure 
reports you'll see 8 configuration files for Oracle in the back, one for 
each instance.  In addition, their "clients" are always configured in 
multiples of 8 which is another hint that partitioning was used.

It is sad that Compaq had to resort to TPC-C tricks like this to try
and show performance on the box.  I imagine the "real" single box 
performance is not terrible, just not leadership.  The NUMA hit on
that box is really much too large to run TPC-C really well with a
single instance.

