"Alexis Cousein" <al@brussels.sgi.com> wrote in message
news:3B4FBD69.4070508@brussels.sgi.com...
> mulp wrote:
> > Instead, the focus should have switched to bringing in EV7/Marvel with
some
> > more bandaid work on the GS140,
> So -- were you suggesting Compaq should have entirely avoided making a
> viable largish platform for the entire EV6 generation of EVx processors
> and just used ES40 for the smaller systems and skipped straight to EV7
> for larger systems?

I'm saying that once wildfire's schedule became so delayed that it was
seriously interfering with moving resources from wildfire to other EV6 and
EV7 related projects, and it was decided that no additional resources were
going to be added to keep those other projects on track, then wildfire
should have been cancelled.

When wildfire was conceived, it made sense.

Wildfire is an enhanced version of the ES40 and what adds all the complexity
is the need to connect switch asics together to increase the number of
ports.  The DS20 uses one set of chips, the ES40 uses four sets.  Those
asics were port limited because of the technology.   Wildfire started with
the same asic generation and scaled from there and the number of asics
exploded in large part because the additional states required spliting the
functions in the ES40 chips over multiple asics which required additional
asics to coordinate them all.

Wildfire in the market a year earlier wouldn't have been better than it is
now but it would have met a real market need.  It would have allowed
reducing the effort spent on tlaser a year earlier, possibly eliminating an
upgrade from that platform.  It probably would have save some accounts which
were lost because tlaser couldn't handle them.  And wildfire resources would
have been free to go onto other work a year earlier.




