>Not necessarily.  True fault-tolerance (the Tandem/Stratus approach, where
>redundant hardware executes in lock-step with comparators 
>checking hardware
>outputs for consistency and failing in various ways - 'pair-and-spare',
>voting, or whatever - when inconsistent) does 'waste' the redundant
>hardware.

Sorry.  That was just my penurious nature talking.  I do understand
that there are people who need this.

>
>But if you assume fail-fast/fail-stop operation 
>(such that any failure will
>shut down the failed component before erroneous results are propagated -
>which can be encouraged, though not ensured, by 
>relatively low-cost embedded
>check mechanisms such as bus-parity and ECC), then you can support more
>loosely-coupled node-to-node redundancy.  

Right.  Doesn't this require some kind of interrupt 
facility so that the OS gets notified? 

> ...And in that case, if you're
>willing to tolerate some loss of system capacity/oerformance when a node
>fails, both nodes can be doing useful work in the non-failure case.  
>Extend
>this to an N-node cluster, and when a node fails you lose only 1/Nth your
>system capacity.

I would choose this.  I think my concern is if any computing
service has to be interrupted.  The earilier the failure is
detected and communicated to the OS, the better.  A trend
that I've been getting a smell of but haven't verified yet,
is that the software side of the biz is assuming that 
hardware never fails.  IF that becomes prevalent in a majority
of installations, it does imply that those failovers have to
be done at the hardware level.  Even if you guys knew
that much magic :-), it still smacks of a "one size fits
all" mentality w.r.t. hardware.  That's extremely distasteful
to me.

/BAH

