> > > tolerance even when most of the nodes don't have direct access to the
> system
> > > disk.
> 
> > For other implementations of Single System Image, you don't need
> > shared anything, including the system disk. I'm thinking
> 
> I guess that Single System Image has as many meaning as Cluster.
> 
> From the context that I heard SSI used, it referred to the changes made to
> the way that Tru64 unix was installed and booted that allowed a cluster to
> run from a single system image - ie the bits on disk.  This was in contrast
> to the prior releases which required a set of bits on unique disks for every
> node in the cluster with unique installs done for each and then a complex
> process of binding all the nodes together.
> 
> You go on to mention a different implementation that I'm sort of at a loss
> to understand.

Well, taking the words literally, the use of a single set of bits
to hold the system image for multiple machines, on a shared disk,
is a kind of single system image (lower case).

What I, and I believe most people, take Single System Image (with
upper case) to mean is "the illusion, created by software or
hardware, that a collection of computing elements is a single
computing resource." (quoted from my book: In Search of Clusters,
2e, page 377.)

Tru64Cluster provides that illusion at the kernel interface: Not
counting performance issues, it's impossible to tell from any
program running under it whether the OS is in fact running on one
computer or distributed across multiple computers.

Most clusters don't do this at the kernel level. Many cluster
applications do, however, provide an SSI as part of the function
of that application. Several databases -- most of them these days
-- also have options that provide this for any program using the
database or any stored procedure. Examples: Oracle Parallel
Server (or RAC), DB2 EEE, DB2 on zSeries and iSeries, Informix
something-or-other.

Greg Pfister


