I think all of the answers to the confusion you are having are in the
various posts, but to try to make things easier, let me try to restate what
they have said on one place.  If I get it wrong, I am sure someone will
correct me.

Write back/write thru - refers to a property of caches.  This is an issue
even on a single CPU machine.  The key issue here is whether a store
instruction causes the updated data in the cache to be written to the next
level of the memory hierarchy. Write thru caches cause that to be done,
essentially as a direct result of the store.  Write back caches will have to
write it back eventually, but will try to delay the write back until
necesary.  Performance is usually better with write back, but it complicates
some error recovery issues.  On a write back cache, invalidating the cache
before the write to the next level of the hierarchy will probably cause data
corruption (unless you know the data won't be used).  On a write thru cache,
invalidation of the data after the store won't cause data coruption because
the data is in the next lowest level, but may not be a good idea for
performance reasons.

Refresh - a property of DRAM chips.  This is invisible to the software
(except prhaps timing) but is known to the circuit board designer.  It is
well explained in Dennis's post

Restore - a property of many kinds of memory systems, including DRAMs and
magnetic core.  It is invisible to the software and to the circuit board
designer (at least for DRAMD) but is important for the guy who designs the
DRAM chips.  For core, it was known to the people who designed the memory
controller, but unknown (again, except for possible timing) to the guys who
designed the CPU.

Cache coherency - an issue for systems with multiple caches.  It is the
question of how, given a store to a cache in one CPU, does another CPU know
that the data has been updated so it doesn't operate on stale data.  There
are several different solutions to this problem.  It is related to the write
back/ write through issue in that the coherency protocol has to be designed
with that in mind.  the refresh and restore have no impact on the coherency
design.

I hope that helps.

--
    -  Stephen Fuld




