My guess is that the time line for doing formal verification of a complex
system would be on the order of 10 times longer than extensive testing.

I am using the word system, not component or algorithm.

When you are designing something that is complex and difficult to test and
that will be put into silicon, then you seriously look at doing formal
verification.  But you also do as much simulation as you can afford.  If you
can't find someone like Lamport, et al, to do the formal verification, then
you had better be able to afford some serious simulation effort.

To formally verify something like VAXclusters V4, well, it still would be a
long way from completion today.  There was, in fact, a project to formally
verify the CI architecture and implementation.  That effort found problems
that were also found in cluster testing, but the cluster testing found
several orders of magnitude more problems with only about 4 times the number
of people and resources, and none of the system test people were phd level
folk.




