"Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> wrote ...
> <jmfbahciv@aol.com> wrote ...
> >    "Ren� Schelbaum" <rene.schelbaum@datakom.at> wrote:
>
> > >In the good old days of core (magnetic) memory the
> > >read operation destroyed
> > >the information, so the cell had to be rewritten after write.
> >
> > Ok.  Now, is the term 'write-back' correct?  If it is correct,
> > then what does it mean now?  People don't use iron core
> > anymore except in special cases...or am I wrong about that, too?
>
> IIRC what you're talking about above is called 'refresh'.

No, it isn't.

DRAM "Refresh" refers to the regeneration of RAM cells
whose charge has degraded, by leakage, back to a strong
0 or 1 charge level. It does not refer to the internal restoration
of a row of bits  after they have been destructively read out:
This destructive readout is not due to leakage, but rather
it is due to the intentional connection of the cell's charge
storage capacitance to the much large capacitance of the
bit line and sense amp input.

In fact, refresh  cycles are, as far as the DRAM array itself
is concerned, just another read operation. Every access that
hits the DRAM's cell array* performs a read, then optionally
a modification of the data that was read out, and then a store
back into the array. In the vast majority of DRAM**s, even
store operations do this,  since they are only modifying a
portion of the selected row .

None of this is restoring of destructively-read-out data
is visible outside the DRAM, except in the timing.

*some DRAMs have small caches, some only the
size of a single row, so not all access to these kinds
of DRAMs will access the actual cell array.

**some specialized forms of DRAMs have write buffers
that are one row in size, into which data is shifted, and
then the buffer is written.  These kinds of DRAMs
do not need to (but might anyway) do read-modify-writes
when writing one of these special buffers.
