"Del Cecchi" <cecchi@signa.rchland.ibm.com> wrote ...
> My explanation of write back vrs write through:  When data that is cached is
> written, the data in main memory may or not be updated simultaneously.  If it is
> updated more or less simultaneously, that is write through (writes through the
> cache to main memory).  Write back delays the update to main memory until the
> line is ejected from the cache.

Del, after reading your explanation, it occurred to me that it
would be better to not bring "time" into the picture.  Let me
try to rework yours to remove the temporal implications:

 "When an inner level of the memory hierarchy stores data into a
  write-thru cache, that store alone will cause the data to also be
  sent to the outer-next level of the memory hierarchy.

 "But when an inner level of the memory hierarchy stores data into
  a write-back cache, that store will not cause the data to be sent
  to the outer-next level of the memory hierarchy: in a write-back
  cache, the data that was stored will not be sent to the outer-next
  level of the memory hierarchy until some other event (such as a
  snoop, an explicit flush operation, or the operation of the cache's
  line replacement algorithm) occurs."

If anyone can make that a little less wordy, they probably should.
