> > If anyone can make that a little less wordy, they probably should.
>
> Shorter, but doesn't explain the underlying h/w mechanisms:
>
> "Suppose a store operation is followed by a cache(line) invalidation.
>
> A write through cache guarantees the corresponding read returns the value
written.
> A write back cache does not."
>

Wrong!!!  A store can only be followed by an "invalidate" if the cache is
write thru.  When the cache line is dirty with a writeback cache and you
need to free the cache line, its called a "victim write" and the data is
written somewhere more persistent, which may or may not be memory.

No matter what kind of cache, a CPU that writes to an address will always
read back the data that it wrote unless that location has been written by
another CPU since both last synchronize memory, in which case the data read
will either be the data written or the data written by the other CPU.




