No, that's actually not always true.  If you can guarantee that you
will never need the data in the write-back cache again, you can
safely invalidate it without flushing it first.  There are actually
quite a few circumstances when you can do this, and I actually had
written them out, and then I decided I might be derelict in my duty
to my employer to post them.  But think about it: when in a program
do you know that you will never need the last results you wrote
