On Fri, 13 Jul 2001, mulp wrote:

> "Peter Boyle" <pboyle@holyrood.ed.ac.uk> wrote in message

> > "Suppose a store operation is followed by a cache(line) invalidation.
> >
> > A write through cache guarantees the corresponding read returns the value
> written.
> > A write back cache does not."

> Wrong!!!  A store can only be followed by an "invalidate" if the cache is
> write thru.

Hi,

No - you can (on PowerPC) invalidate whenever using the dcbi instruction.

The results are, of course, only "sensible" if the cache is write through
and that is both my point, and the reason it is a priveledged instruction.

> When the cache line is dirty with a writeback cache and you
> need to free the cache line, its called a "victim write" and the data is
> written somewhere more persistent, which may or may not be memory.

That is a flush (or eviction) rather than an invalidate.

> No matter what kind of cache, a CPU that writes to an address will always
> read back the data that it wrote unless that location has been written by
> another CPU since both last synchronize memory, in which case the data read
> will either be the data written or the data written by the other CPU.

at I am using a embedded PPC board right now:
If I set up the tlb to map 0xDE000000 as writeback and 0xB0100000
as write through and run the following:


	lis %r11,0xDE00 ! Write back
	lis %r12,0xB010 ! Write through

	li %r9,0x0F
	li %r10,0xFF

	stw %r9,0(%r11) ! Stores 0x0F
	stw %r9,0(%r12)

	sync
	dcbf %r0,%r11   ! 0x00 Makes it back to mem
	dcbf %r0,%r12
 	sync

	stw %r10,0(%r11)! Stores 0xFF
	stw %r10,0(%r12)

	sync
	dcbi %r0,%r11   ! Invalidation kills the write-back copy
	dcbi %r0,%r12   ! But the write through already made it
 	sync

	lwz %r14,0(%r11) ! reads in the memory
	lwz %r15,0(%r12) ! and gets different answers

It ends up with:

 %r14 = 0x0F
 %r15 = 0xFF

Now for the jokes about coherent arguments....

Peter





