On 12 Jul 2001 17:11:14 -0500, Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam
(Larry Kilgallen) wrote:

>In article <3b4e069b.88035413@News.CIS.DFN.DE>, iddw@hotmail.com (Dave Hansen) writes:
>
>> "Testing is the process of validating software."
>> 
>> "Testing is verifying that a program meets its requirements."
>
>Well, I suppose when you are ready to ship, running all your collected
>tests to ensure there are no regressions falls into that category, but
>that is just test execution, which should be an automated no-brainer.

I disagree that regression testing is any different from any other
kind of testing in this respect.  

You test to find bugs.  You have limited resources, so you want to
perform the tests that are most likely to find bugs.  You run
regression tests because empirical evidence shows that bugs that have
been "fixed" tend to "reappear" in later revisions.  Therefore,
regression tests are likely to find bugs.

I do agree that regression tests should be automated.  As a former
employee of a company that builds automated test sytems, I tend to
think you should automate all testing as much as possible.
Particularly since I bought some of my options when I left that
company.  ;-)

>
>They are also correct from the 50,000 foot level, up where managers
>believe no bugs will be found :-)

Well, they are *perceived* as correct, but I claim they aren't.
Again, they reduce testing to a hurdle to be overcome when it should
be seen as a value-added activity.  As Bill Todd said in another post,
finding bugs should be viewed positively.  

>
>The tricky part is figuring out which bugs to fix in the end game --
>which are critical enough to risk destabilizing the product.

Yes.  Testing finds the bugs.  What you do about them is a
higher-level issue.  Remember the programmer's drinking song:

  100 little bugs in the code
  100 bugs in the code
  Fix one bug and recompile
  101 little bugs in the code...

Regards,

                               -=Dave
-- 
Change is inevitable, progress is not.


