"Konrad Schwarz" <konradDOTschwarz@mchpDOTsiemens.de> wrote in message
news:3B4B24A5.5E40E2F3@mchpDOTsiemens.de...
> 2. Cache coherency on SMP is achieved using snooping: the CPU/cache's
> bus interface "snoops" for requests for memory lines cached within
> it; modified lines are either written out to main memory and the
> access retried or the cache serves the memory request directly.

There is no requirement to implement snoopy cache invalidates and even UMA
SMP systems as well as NUMA SMP systems use other methods.  Wildfire
implements writeback cache for the host end of the I/O hose which operates
the same way as the writeback cache used by CPUs.  (This is certainly the
first implementation within DEC and at the time, no one knew of another
implementation elsewhere.)

Just as the CPUs have writeback cache ownerships like readonly, shared-read,
write-access, modified, etc. the I/O controller did the same thing with the
additional complexity of doing prefetches for outbound DMAs and write behind
on inbound DMAs.  To further complicate things, PCI bus transactions have
implicit memory barrier semantics, so the distributed I/O logic has to do
things in such a way to provide the memory barrier semantics without
incurring all the penalty that forcing visibility on every transaction would
imply.  On the CPU side, memory barriers were made visible to and forced on
the software, much to the consternation of the software folk.  (By the way,
IA64 takes a similar approach, so in moving from Alpha to IA64 you can
naively introduce obscure bugs by doing a global replace of MB with mf.)




