In article <9ik7eb$1s3$1@bob.news.rcn.net>, jmfbahciv@aol.com writes:
> In article <9ii2dg$jcr$1@pyrite.mv.net>,
>> ... The
>>cache hardware in each processor snoops the memory bus (or uses other
>>mechanisms) such that it knows if some other processor is requesting data
>>that's dirty in its own local cache and makes sure 
>>that the other processor gets the most up-to-date version 
>>of that data.
> 
> Is this checking really more efficient than just simply
> doing a write-through?  

It really depends. The snooping itself doesn't take a lot of bus bandwidth,
but a lot depends on what happens when a cache decides it has newer data
than memory.

On Firefox everything was built around the CVAX pin-bus. If one of the 
bus interfaces discovered that it had newer data than memory, it had to
perform a DMA request to kick the CPU off the bus so it could fetch the
modified data. This, combined with the CQBIC's lack of on-board scatter/
gather map (it has a scatter/gather map cache and fetches entries from
main memory if the required entry isn't in the cache) is what killed
FTAM. If all the planets lined up correctly, FTAM could require up to
12 microseconds just to get ready for the QBus transaction it was trying
to handle, which isn't a good thing for a bus with a transaction timeout
of 8 microseconds. FQAM dealt with this by having an on-board scatter/gather
map and keeping the bus 100% busy with known, short transactions while the
QBus was granted to a DMA device. This meant the system did _nothing_ else
while QBus DMA was active, because FQAM couldn't afford to let the system
do anything else.  It also gave FQAM it's reputation of being a pig.

In addition to all this, Firefox didn't have an invalid state; you couldn't
invalidate a cache entry. This meant that once something got into a cache
(where it was really slow to fetch the data), it was very difficult to
get it back out.

If, on the other hand, the cache controller has a private datapath to the
cache memory, it can be quite speedy and efficient.

> Doing it the way you described above
> sounds like there's a potential of a lot of CPU contention
> between CPUs.  If not carefully handled, this could make
> some very interest CATCH-22s.  

Absolutely.
