> >[snip]
> >3. Nobody (except Intel) has atomic read/modify/write instructions.
>
> Ever heard of Test-and-Set (S/360)?  Compare-and-Swap (S/370 etc.)  PLO
> (Perform Locked Operation, S/390)?  VAX also has atomic list-update
operations
> that are in the spirit of PLO (synchronisation using microcode locks
invisible
> to the programmer); TS and CS use hardware synchronisation.

Perhaps the intent was to refer to the ability to modify one byte in a word
in thread 1 while thread 2 modifies a second byte in the same work on a
different CPU.

Windows apps are often multithreaded and often have data structures with
adjacent byte fields that are modified by different threads without any
interlocking.  Because there is no practical way for a compiler to determine
whether a second of code might be executed in a threaded environment, the
Alpha compilers had to blindly generate atomic read/modify/write sequences
because there was no support in the hardware.

Note that the ability to modify a two bytes in a word on two different CPUs
doesn't have anything to do with data visibility.  The CPU/memory system has
to correctly update the bytes, but there is nothing that says that if I
write byte 1 after you wrote byte 0, that I will be able to see the value
you wrote into byte 0, even when the granuality of the memory system is 64
bytes.




