"Roger Ivie" <ivie@cc.usu.edu> wrote in message
news:kJzsKDXxW$TE@cc.usu.edu...
> In article <9ik7r7$1s3$3@bob.news.rcn.net>, jmfbahciv@aol.com writes:
> > Ok.  Now, is the term 'write-back' correct?  If it is correct,
> > then what does it mean now?
>
> I don't know what term was used then, but now write-back means that the
> CPU writes data to the cache instead of main memory. The cache will,
> sometime later, make sure the data gets to memory. Usually, this is done
> when the cache line needs to be re-used. The caches in the system
> cooperate to make sure the CPUs and I/O devices see the modified data
> from the cache instead of stale data from memory.

Actually, writeback is probably still used to describe cache systems that
only do "writeback" as a last resort.  Wildfire, and I'm sure other numa
systems, forward the data from one CPU's cache to one or more other CPUs
without going to memory, sometime retaining a copy and other times not.
Only a evict of a non-shared cache line results in a victim write back to
memory.

To make systems like this work, cache lines are tagged with states like
    read
    shared read
    shared modified
    shared write
    exclusive write
    exclusive modified
Only exclusive modified would require a write back to memory; evicting a
cache line that is shared modifed might be implemented by sending the
modified data to other other CPUs that have the data.




