<jmfbahciv@aol.com> wrote in message news:9ik6nh$rll$1@bob.news.rcn.net...

...

> Consider a company whose business is to deliver uninterrupted
> telephone service.  Hmmm...I'm talking myself out of my argument.
> This business wants both.  This isn't an easy problem to solve :-).
> Those guys want an invisible failover process.  That means
> redundancy.  Eh!  What a waste of computes and storage.

Not necessarily.  True fault-tolerance (the Tandem/Stratus approach, where
redundant hardware executes in lock-step with comparators checking hardware
outputs for consistency and failing in various ways - 'pair-and-spare',
voting, or whatever - when inconsistent) does 'waste' the redundant
hardware.

But if you assume fail-fast/fail-stop operation (such that any failure will
shut down the failed component before erroneous results are propagated -
which can be encouraged, though not ensured, by relatively low-cost embedded
check mechanisms such as bus-parity and ECC), then you can support more
loosely-coupled node-to-node redundancy.  And in that case, if you're
willing to tolerate some loss of system capacity/oerformance when a node
fails, both nodes can be doing useful work in the non-failure case.  Extend
this to an N-node cluster, and when a node fails you lose only 1/Nth your
system capacity.

- bill

