On Thu, 12 Jul 2001 16:22:28 GMT, "mulp"
<michaelpettengill@earthlink.net> wrote:

[...]
>
>Here's another book to look for at the library:
> Software Testing in the Real World: improving the process
> by Edward Kit
>ISBN 0-201-87756-2

I haven't read Kit's book, but I've heard good things about it.  Two
more: 

   "The Art of Software Testing" by Glenford Myers

   "Testing Computer Software (2nd Ed.)" by Kaner, Falk, and Nguyen

>
>I've only skipped around in the book but I found enough gems to cause me to
>steal the book from a coworker.  I would replace the "software" in the title
>with "system".
>
>Some of the gems:
> Testers are destructive - but creatively so
> Testers pursue errors, not people
> Testers add value

This ^ is the one I have trouble convincing people of.  Testing is far
too often seen a a hurdle to be overcome rather than a value-added
activity...

> Testers hunt errors
>
>This book emphasizes that testers don't prove that a product is bug free,
>which is what devos and engineering and product managers want to hear.
>

Both the books I listed make pretty much the same points.  They tend
to support each other.  

Myers says "Testing is the process of executing a program with the
intent of finding errors."  Seems obvious until you find out how many
people don't believe it.

Kaner et al says "The purpose of testing a program is to find problems
in it."  Good stuff.  

Regards,

                               -=Dave
-- 
Change is inevitable, progress is not.


