>There is a big difference. 

>SGI is committed to continued development of the MIPS/IRIX platform. 
>Compaq has cancelled future alpha development beyond EV7.
>SGI views MIPS/IRIX as it's core business.
>Compaq view alpha as a distraction/money sink that they needed to ditch.


Fine.  A month before Compaq ditched Alpha, they published a roadmap (of
sorts) with details through EV8 and hand-waving up through EV12 in 2009
or so.  Anyone can produce a roadmap and say they are "committed".  Next
week SGI could change their mind and say the R16K will be the last MIPS
design they use and they are moving to IA64, and justify it in any of the
several ways Compaq did.  Plus one other one Compaq couldn't use -- that
they are nearing bankruptcy and need to trim costs aggressively to insure
the survival of the business.  Brannon Batson would have laughed at any
suggestion that Compaq would abandon Alpha until he found out.  Hopefully
he learned it before the rumours at The Inquirer were posted the weekend
before Compaq announced it, but considering the way Compaq handled it, I
wouldn't be surprised if he was caught off guard just as much Compaq's
customers were.

I'm sure you really believe what you say, and no one is accusing you of
being untruthful.  We just don't believe that SGI will follow through on
this, especially given their aggressive efforts to move towards a Linux/
IA-64 makeup.  I think SGI would be more successful as the "supercomputer
company behind high performance Linux/IA-64 solutions" than they would be
trying to push lower end RISC solutions with a proprietary Unix.  The
fact that SGI's big systems do so well despite being handicapped by the
laggard MIPS processors shows how well SGI designs those big systems, so
presumably they could design the highest performance big IA-64 systems
around.  There'd certainly be a large market for that, perhaps even
outside their traditional scientific and video/graphics market, into the
lucrative enterprise systems market where they are pretty much a
nonfactor today.

