It's possible you're just completely unfamiliar with how modern SMPs work
(Greg Pfister's "In Search of Clusters" IIRC has a good introductory section
on this topic, and a great deal of other good background as well).  The
cache hardware in each processor snoops the memory bus (or uses other
mechanisms) such that it knows if some other processor is requesting data
that's dirty in its own local cache and makes sure that the other processor
gets the most up-to-date version of that data.

This means that cached dirty data can be written back (rather than written
through) to memory to obtain performance advantages (coalescence of multiple
updates to data in the same cache line) without compromising the distributed
coherence of the system (the performance impact of ping-ponging cached data
between multiple updating processors varies with different implementations,
but applies only when actual contention for a cache line occurs rather than
impacting every update as write-through caching does).

- bill

