jmfbahciv@aol.com writes:
> Last night, I seemed to remember some hardware type talking
> about how fetching something caused that location in memory
> to change (this was at the electronics level) so the original
> sense of the bits had to be restored.

Core memory has destructive readout.  A normal read cycle includes
a write cycle to restore the value that was read.

Some systems, including PDP-11s, support non-restoring read cycles for
use when the processor knows that the location's contents don't need
to be preserved.  This is generally used for read-modify-write
instructions.  Without such support, the memory would see a read,
a write of the original data, and a write of the new data.  With the
optimization, the unnecessary write of the original data is omitted.

Bulk semiconductor memory is usually DRAM, which needs to be refreshed
periodically.  Typically DRAM is read one entire row at a time  For the
4K*1 parts that were originally used in the MF20 semiconductor memory
system for the KL10, this means that each time the memory chip is asked
to read one bit, sixty-four are internally read, but only one is output.
However, all sixty-four bits are refreshed as part of the read cycle.

The memory refresh process is accomplished by reading all of the rows
of each DRAM within a certain period of time.  In order to refresh multiple
banks in parallel, there are refresh-only cycles that are similar to
read cycles but do not output any data.  Fancier DRAMs have onboard
refresh row counters; really fancy ones have automatic refresh modes.

Because each read cycle reads an entire row within the chip, but only
presents a few of those bits as output, most DRAMs provide a way to
subsequently access other bits of the same row.  The most common method
at that time was page mode, but there were some other variants including
nibble mode and static column mode.  State-of-the art today includes
SDRAM, DDR SDRAM, and RDRAM, all of which have substantially more complex
interfaces than the traditional DRAMs.

Modern DRAM chips are typically 8 to 32 bits wide, but they have row
sizes in the thousands of bits, so it is still more efficient to do
multiple cycles within a row rather than random access.  Thus most
systems attempt to fill an entire cache line in one read burst.


