> mistakes are made.  I bet that you have invested in some stocks that
didn't
> go up as much as you thought they would.
The problem with this analysis is that the MBA looked at DEC as a bundle of
stocks that included the following list:
    Hardware
    VMS software
    VMS languages mainline
    VMS languages low volume *
    VMS RDB *
    VMS transaction management *
    VMS system management tools *
    VMS development tools *
    VMS middleware *
    hardware service contracts
    VMS software service contracts
    DEC consulting services
They concluded that the items marked with * were not generating enough
profit (they were profitable) nor likely to grow into billion dollar
businesses, but they did require a large number of employees - the revenue
per employee was bringing down the average.

Unfortunately, the low volume languages were critical to certain customers
who sold very expensive solutions involving lots of hardware and other
software.  The lack of clear support caused these customers to look to other
platforms.

The system management tools were sold at "cost" to customers buying
consulting services which provided automated, lights out solutions.  Once
sold to CA, CA raised the prices significantly and then when it was clear
that the VMS volume wasn't close to Windows volume for the same products,
they stopped production.  The DEC consulting service folk had to find new
solutions to replace the ones they had developed to provide those services.
The VMS software services contracts shifted to CA, eliminating a significant
profit from Rando's world

The middleware was almost the same situation - with the result that DEC
consulting services lost a lot of business.

VMS RDB was sold to oracle who immediately raised the prices all around and
then took over the accounts to sell Oracle consulting services where
previously DEC consulting services were sold.  Also, the VMS software
service contracts shifted to Oracle, eliminating a significant profit from
Rando's world.

Gee, it turns out that the MBA's view of the world is wrong.  There are a
lot of cross dependencies between these lines of business.

Oh, and better yet, DEC needed software products to run its business.  After
the sale of a number of these products, all the different groups needing the
software would have needed to pay an amount of money equal to the cost of
the engineering groups to keep current with the software.  The result of
that was that IM froze the systems running major parts of the business to
the then current release of VMS, locking a lot of systems into VAX.
Currently, IM uses VMS V6.2 for the many of the remaining production VMS
systems.

I'm pretty sure that this was what happened at IBM around the same time, but
someone at IBM figured out what was going wrong.  Since then, IBM has
returned to the chip fab business, and refocused on chip design, has
invested in basic research leading to advanced chip tech.

I'm hoping that IBM will continue to strive to become the leader in chip
technology and provide the alternative that DEC's chip operations once
provided to do leadership work.




