I have different information on the NSK side.  One project was just getting
ready to code in 98 when they were redirected to Alpha.  They were just
getting ready to code.  Back to ground zero.

There is a lot of information that you need in your head to be able to make
the design decisions that come up every day.  The information builds layer
on layer.  You have to be able to peel away all the layers and do it
accurately to figure out exactly why you have to do it this way rather than
that way.  If you have kept detailed minutes and notes, you might have 10s
or 100s of thousands of notes, memos, mail messages, minutes, and a smaller
amount of specs that are the best effort at capturing the key features of
the design.

If everyone is around and everyone has retained all that information, then
you could go thru all that information and reimmerse yourself in that
information.  But, people leave, information that doesn't seem useful is
discarded, specs that are incomplete are abandoned.  So you have to do more
than just read the old information.

But, more important, in the process of going thru the design process a
second time, and having more information about the environment (Alpha is
still a lot better understood than IA64 is today and 3 years ago the info on
IA64 was much less), you will generally make a different set of choices when
tackling the same problem.





