"Ren� Schelbaum" <rene.schelbaum@datakom.at> wrote ...
> In the good old days of core (magnetic) memory the read operation destroyed
> the information, so the cell had to be rewritten after write.

Semiconductor DRAMs, all kinds, do that as well.

Your basic DRAM read cycle is: charge the bit lines to a
value right at the trip point of your sense amps, activate the
word line to connect the charge-storing capacitance in the
cells to the bit lines, wait a little bit, and see how the voltage
on the bit lines changed.  This causes the capacitance in
the cells you read out to drop or rise to nearly the same
voltage as the bit-line precharge, which is neither a good
one or zero.  So, after you've read the values, you turn
around and write them back in by driving the bit lines
with the (I assume latched) value from the sense amps
(or it's inverse, in which case you kept a bit per word
line that told you whether the data was inverted or not.

Some of the newer RAM technologies (like ferroelectric
RAMs and Flash) don't have this charge-drain problem,
I believe.  And of course, standard 4- or 6-T static RAM
doesn't either.
