"Greg Pfister" <pfister@us.ibm.com> wrote in message
news:3B4B51FB.548A7545@us.ibm.com...
> Also: One thing I've never been quite clear on: Is VMS a complete
> kernel-level single system image? Another way to put it: Is the
> single-system aspect implemented *completely* at an OS level, so
> that all the name spaces used all the kernel calls are globalized
> across the cluster, with all that implies? The Locus line does
> that, providing shared pipes, named segments, etc. (No, shared
> segments don't perform all that well with processes on different
> nodes. Could with hardware support, in theory, but then you're
> getting into NUMA or sCOMA.)

Things that have a unique name are generally made visible across the cluster
in single name space.  Storage devices have unique names as a requirement,
so they global in the device namespace.  (PC hardware, however, was/is
outside the architecture, so those don't have unique names and often end up
being visiable only on the node they attach to.)

Locks are global.

Mailboxes/pipes weren't global until recently.  The reason is that they are
created as devices very early on in the boot process so they end up with
conflicting names like MBA0:.  Further, the semantics are not easily
implemented clusterwide.  And to provide a convenient handle for
applications, the mailboxes have an associated logical name which, until
recently was local to a node.  The reason for that is that logical names
need to be created with local values and created early in the boot process,
well before the other nodes in the cluster have been discovered.

One of the key characteristics of the cluster support for shared objects is
that the application doesn't have to deal with the loss of one node in the
cluster in most cases.  A clusterwide pipe or mailbox is a bit of problem -
what happens when you are sending a chunk of data thru the pipe and the node
on the receiving end goes away - restarting a reader, or having a shared
reader take over, could easily result in a partial message or whatever.  For
the most part, clusters were slipped in under existing apps without them
knowing anything about clusters.

And in the past five years, the emphasis has been making VMS friendlier to
typical "C code" and that code is written with a least common denominator
view of the world, so adding enhanced features to VMS is not going to
enhance VMS as a platform.




