<jbs@watson.ibm.com> wrote in message
news:20010710.173134.784@yktvmv.WATSON.IBM.COM...
> In article <9idnuc$jfg$1@sunnews.cern.ch>,
>  on 10 Jul 2001 02:06:04 GMT,
>  Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>
>          I think this is wrongheaded.  In my experience it is much
> easier to find bugs in other people's code if you have access to
> the source and I expect this outweighs any "bias".

For 14 years my group found lots of really complex bugs in all sorts of
hardware, firmware, and software without any code.

The mindset of most people is that you can test or examine the code and
remove all or most of the bugs from it.

My mindset is that the system will be riddled with bugs, but the only bugs
that matter are the ones that customers see.  A lot of the bugs are in the
exception handling code, and often the bugs relate to misunderstandings of
what is happening at the interface between two components.

It is rare that someone can study a device driver, look at a hardware spec,
and then figure out what the interactions between them is going to be, even
assuming that the spec is correct.

Distributed code/algorithms are also very hard.  The people doing the
implementation usually have a tenuous hold on all the distributed state and
interactions while they are writing and looking at the code.  For someone to
test it with access to the code, they will probably create a simple model of
what's going on and then craft simple tests to exercise the obvious stuff.




