In article <9ieg7h$qp1$1@news.qld.csiro.au>, "Matthew van de Werken"
<m.vandewerken@cat.csiro.au> wrote:

> I have inherited an alpha 3000/400, which won't boot as far as I can tell.
> It goes through all the POST stuff, then says no keyboard/ptr device, and
> goes no further. I have plugged in a k/b and mouse to the machine, so I
> don't understand why this message comes up.
> 
> I have no doco for this machine, so any help is greatly appreciated.

A few things to check...

Do you have the KB and mouse plugged in to the right place?  This machine
requires a special adapter cable.  The KB and mouse plug into a block at
one end of the cable, and the other end of the cable (15 pins IIRC) plugs
into the DEC 3000.

The system will only check for KB and mouse at power-up.

There is a small toggle switch on the back labled "S3".  It should be UP
to use the KB, mouse, and graphics screen as the console.  If it is DOWN,
the system will be the terminal port as the console.  The terminal port is
a 6-pin MMJ port.  S3 is only checked at power-up.

Since you are getting console output, you probably have S3 set right.

If the KB and mouse tests still fail, you either have a bad KB and mouse,
a bad connection, a cabling error, or a sick serail controller in the
system.  You may have to use a terminal as a console.

I don't think the system will auto-boot if the KB and mouse tests fail. 
If you can type commands at the >>> prompt, you can try the boot command. 
You might need to change the environment variables for the default boot
device and/or the boot flags (or override the defaults with the boot
command).  You can also type HELP at the >>> prompt for a short summary of
commands.

I have a 268 kB PDF file, which is the Owner's Guide for the DEC 3000-600
and -700 systems.  These are the successors to the -400.  The manual is
extremely similar to the printed DEC 3000-400 manual I have seen.  I can
email the PDF as an attachment if it would help.

I have also captured several console dialogs with these systems.  If
examples from a working system would be useful, I can email some of those
as well.

