jmfbahciv@aol.com schrieb:
> >> Wait a minute...There's a difference between write-back and
> >> write-through.  A write-back means that the cache memeory
> >> doesn't change if it gets touched.  The write-through means
> >> that what gets changed in the cache is guaranteed to get
> >> changed in the memory _at the hardware level_ and doesn't
> >> require smart programs to ensure data integrity.
> >
> >Yes and no - write-back cache does not immediately write back data,
> 
> But it has to write back immediately with
> read-modify-write instructions.  Am I tripping
> over my ignorance about today's hardware?

1. Symmetric MP means that memory and IO looks the same from all
CPUs.  This is easy to achieve with bus-based systems: all
CPUs hang off the same bus as memory and the memory-based IO.  Routing
interrupts properly is more diffult and requires special HW support.
(This means that one and only one CPU's interrupt request line
is asserted for any specific interrupt request.  However,
each CPU must be able to handle any interrupt request in the system;
otherwise the system is no longer symmetric.)

2. Cache coherency on SMP is achieved using snooping: the CPU/cache's
bus interface "snoops" for requests for memory lines cached within
it; modified lines are either written out to main memory and the
access retried or the cache serves the memory request directly.

3. Nobody (except Intel) has atomic read/modify/write instructions.
(Some embedded processors also have swap, but this is insufficient for
efficient scheduling, to my knowlege.)
Instead, they have load-locked/store conditional instruction sequences
which can emulate r/m/w using a loop.   Synchronization is achieved
using a variant of the mutex/event variable (or binary semaphore
initialized to zero/one) "patterns".  Mutual exclusion between
processors is done using spin locks: using l-l/s-c until the 
store succeeds.  Propagating events must be done by asserting
interrupts, but I don't know if this is really necessary.

4. Snooping ensures spin lock memory consistency.

5. NUMA is more complicated; I can't tell you how it works.


