Even with one file you can take advantage of parallel compilation.
E.g., if you use GCC it runs "cpp0" (preprocessor), "cc1" (compiler
proper) and "as" (assembler).  With "-pipe", the output from one is
piped to the next.  The linker runs separately at the end though.

I imagine that something similar could easily be implemented in other
compilers like GEM, preferably using a binary format for speed instead
of parsing ASCII several times like GCC.

