At 5000 RPM 0.01s is 20 revolutions. Your point about the
way EPA measures emissions is fine. You may even make a point
that with injectors dead the flywheel pumps clean air through
your cat and you won't even get a backfire. But this certainly
is a major sputter that customers will find hard to accept.

Also, upon the reboot the computer will need to pick up the
speed. I do not expect it realistically do it. At least in
my car, the PCM goes to default mapping after a reboot and
it takes some time for it to get sustained readings from
O2 sensors, MAP sensor, TPS sensor, etc. (less time to
pick crankshaft position and rotation sensor though - about 0.005 s
ought to be enough). I expect a couple of seconds before
computer figures what is happening with the engine,
during which 1. power is suboptimal 2. emissions are
seriously below standard.

My point is that computer reboots in a car are not
acceptable, even if they take 0.01s.

By the way, my car push starts _very_ easily, from which
I deduce that computer reboots in under less than 0.5s
from the moment the alternator supplies power. Still,
I would not like PCM to die while I negotiate a turn.
A spin due to drive wheels traction loss is likely.

-- Pete


