In article <3B4B24A5.5E40E2F3@mchpDOTsiemens.de>,
Konrad Schwarz  <konradDOTschwarz@mchpDOTsiemens.de> wrote:
>[snip]
>2. Cache coherency on SMP is achieved using snooping: [...]

How about "can be achieved"!  There are other mechanisms: directory-based
(often used in NUMA systems), or point-to-point signalling (S/370 and its
descendants).

>3. Nobody (except Intel) has atomic read/modify/write instructions.

Ever heard of Test-and-Set (S/360)?  Compare-and-Swap (S/370 etc.)  PLO
(Perform Locked Operation, S/390)?  VAX also has atomic list-update operations
that are in the spirit of PLO (synchronisation using microcode locks invisible
to the programmer); TS and CS use hardware synchronisation.

I'm sure there are others -- the concept is nearly 40 years old.  (Anybody
here from what used to be Burroughs?)

>(Some embedded processors also have swap, but this is insufficient for
>efficient scheduling, to my knowlege.)

Single-word Compare-and-Swap is indeed insufficient, which is why S/370 also
has Compare-Double-and-Swap (allowing both address and key to be handled in
one atomic update).

>Instead, they have load-locked/store conditional instruction sequences
>which can emulate r/m/w using a loop.   [...]

The nice thing about this newer way of doing things is that it is more general
as well as more primitive, and can be used to synthesize many different higher-
level synchronisation mechanisms.

Michel.


