> 
> For VMS, all that is needed to run "single system image" is for each node to
> somehow have access to the same system disk.  This can be done with fault
> tolerance even when most of the nodes don't have direct access to the system
> disk.
> 
> The way this is accomplished is by having two or more nodes with direct
> access to the storage.

For other implementations of Single System Image, you don't need
shared anything, including the system disk. I'm thinking
particularly of the line of systems that started with Locus
project at UCLA, went into some unlamented (for other reasons)
versions of Un*x on S/390 and PS/2, from there to Intel iPSC,
then to Tandem, then via Compaq to DEC Tru64Cluster, and most
recently to Linux - GPL open source. (I think I got all the ones
that appeared in public...)

At least the later implementations of this can use shared disk
access (for data & system), but do not require it. In its heart
it's a shared-nothing implementation: Plug your systems into the
same LAN, and off you go.

Shared devices are not required for SSI. Shared devices aren't
required for HA, either (you can mirror). Whether they make the
resultant system better in some ways (load balancing, overhead,
etc.) is something that can be debated ad nauseam, and has been.

Also: One thing I've never been quite clear on: Is VMS a complete
kernel-level single system image? Another way to put it: Is the
single-system aspect implemented *completely* at an OS level, so
that all the name spaces used all the kernel calls are globalized
across the cluster, with all that implies? The Locus line does
that, providing shared pipes, named segments, etc. (No, shared
segments don't perform all that well with processes on different
nodes. Could with hardware support, in theory, but then you're
getting into NUMA or sCOMA.)

Greg Pfister


