Paul Brown  <pol@read.the.sig.coz.this.address.is.invalid> wrote:
>> [big snip]
>>> >in fact it doesn't/shouldn't write it back unless there is a need, but
>>> >all write-back caches still have the hardware that ensures the results do
>>> >go out of teh cache if needed (coherence).
>>> 
>>> Sure.  But a write-through cache ensures that anything modified
>>> in the cache is also in memory. 
>
>> Or into the next level of cache. This is used in zSeries - first
>> level cache is write-through, second level is write back, and
>> shared among >1 processors. That's part of how a processor can
>> die without the whole system dying: Other processors have access
>> to what it changed in memory through the second level cache.
>
>> (Yes, the second level cache should be made bulletproof if this
>> is part of what you're using it for.)
>
>What about registers?

Essentially, the systems are self-checkpointing in cases
like this.  If you think about it, a checkpoint is freezing
the process image so that you can restore operation to that
point if you have to.  With a cache, any operations that
operate in the cache and registers only effectively leave
what's in main memory as a frozen checkpoint.  Nothing "matters"
until it gets back out to where it's recorded and other CPUs
can see it.  Another processor can pick up PC and the contents
of main memory and run with it if the current running CPU goes
away gracefully without writing out any corrupted data...


