I think you are splitting hairs. Alpha effectively died with Compaq's
announcement. Alpha is a marginal player ISA whose claim to fame is
implementations with high, generally unmatched performance. Unlike
MIPS and PowerPC it has no escape route to embedded control. Alpha
lives only because the next implementation promises what no one
else can deliver in the same time span. Compaq effectively stopped
work on EV8 and announced that the Alpha design group would be
gradually transferred to Intel to work on non-Alpha MPUs as they
finished their role in delivering the EV7 to market. For any third
party to think they could round up a few Alpha designers and continue
in a concerted, directed fashion at keeping the architecture at the
forefront is ludicrous. With no future at the performance leading edge
beyond EV7 and no escape to embedded control simply means Alpha is
dead in several years.

