In article <3B4B51FB.548A7545@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:

> Also: One thing I've never been quite clear on: Is VMS a complete
> kernel-level single system image? Another way to put it: Is the
> single-system aspect implemented *completely* at an OS level, so
> that all the name spaces used all the kernel calls are globalized
> across the cluster, with all that implies? The Locus line does
> that, providing shared pipes, named segments, etc. (No, shared
> segments don't perform all that well with processes on different
> nodes. Could with hardware support, in theory, but then you're
> getting into NUMA or sCOMA.)

As you probably have figured out, the term "single system image"
is foreign to VMS folk, and putting together the meanings of the
individual words the natural VMS reaction is to presume one means
a shared system disk.

But based on your description of "kernel-level single system image",
the answer is "No".  VMS mailboxes do not cross node boundaries.
The only sharing of global sections between systems is within
a Galaxy.  There is no attempt to simulate shared memory where
in fact no shared memory is present.  I believe the reasoning was
that the only reason people were using global sections was for
the performance benefit, so absolutely nobody would find simulated
shared memory acceptable.

As for "name spaces", those are global across the cluster,
although in some cases abbreviation leads to the "local"
resource.  I can refer to a disk as MYNODE$DKA300: or DKA300:
if it is on my node, even in kernel mode.


