Until 2 weeks ago, VMS was rather inextricably intertwined with Alpha and
Tru64, sharing, e.g., a great deal of compiler development resources.  Now
Alpha is not an issue in that sense, but anyone buying VMS would have to
trust Compaq to continue to support Alpha for the duration of any porting
effort and have to trust both Compaq and Intel to continue to support their
respective VMS compilers (unless they bought Tru64 plus the remaining
relevant compilers owned by Compaq).

Then there's the service organization, which is critical to VMS's main
mid-range-and-higher markets.  Since Compaq claims it's heading in the
service direction, it likely wouldn't want to part with it, hence you'd have
to trust Compaq in that area as well.

It would probably make more sense for someone interested in this area to
purchase Compaq as a whole, get rid of its current management, and try to
make a go of it from that standpoint.  Too bad that's not what happened when
Compaq purchased DEC (I still wonder if that's what Pfeiffer had in mind
before getting tossed out for PC-area problems which may or may not have
justified his ouster).

