Folks have noticed that prices for used alphas are somewhat lower during
the summer months.  Students and other university folks are off doing
other things, and are less interested in geeking around.

My own limited observations tend to agree with this view.

Of course, there is a general downward trend for all used computer
equipment, and it's fairly rapid.


> > What is interesting is that the higher end equipment no one is even
> > bidding on it at the starting prices.

There are one or two sellers who periodically flood ebay with wildly
overpriced alpha systems.  Their starting prices don't convey much useful
information.

If a seller offers to give the money back if the system arrives dead, if
he says it is working at some minimal level, and if he describes it in
detail, it will bring a good price.  If he fails to do any of these
things, it will likely knock the price in half, or worse.

There are also bargain-hunters, who try to read between the lines and find
unpolished gems.  A part number for a rare item, but no name.  An
expensive component barely discernable in a photo, but not mentioned at
all in the text.  There are folks who notice such things, and take
advantage of the ill-informed sellers.

There are certain alpha-related items I've paid attention to at ebay for a
year or more, and the quality of the seller is an important factor in the
final price.

