> Does this really work?  We've been through a couple forced technology
> mergers at my software job, and the results have been mixed at best.
> The only hardware technology merger from a failed project that comes to
> mind is the recycling of the Motorola 88K bus interface for use in
> PowerPC.  Was that considered a long-term beneficial combination?

Intel has done this, successfully, before.

E.g. the Intel 960 group - rabid RISC advocates, the first superscalar
microprocessor (probably also, for that matter, the first multithreaded
processor) - built the Intel P6 processor family, Intel's first, and one of
the world's first, out-of-order microprocessors.

I suspect that some of the reasons that the P6 was successful included
(a) no baggage - no preconceived ideas of how to implement an x86
(b) no maintenance - the group was not preoccupied maintaining designs
    already in the field

I suspect that the Digital/Compaq design teams, whatever Intel instruction
set they work on, will have these advantages. I look forward to seeing their
designs.  I'm more than a little bit jealous.



