Just to check these 47 year old brain cells -- I think I'm missing 
something here... perhaps it's in the description.

Here's the field service recollection from my 8600 training in '85/86.

Write-back cache was first implemented at DEC in either the 8600 Vax
or KL10 (iirc).  Write-back means that the cache and memory controller
would update memory to reflect cache changes.

Prior to that Write-through cache was used -- this meant a change in
memory would go directly to memory and the cache data pointing to the
memory address would be invalidated.

Am I correct?

The Mbox on the 8600/8650 was a bear for the designers to get correct
cache and memory timing problems (iirc) for the memory control
delayed the 8650 (which was the original 8600's speed plan)
and had the 8600 come out slow and late.

