> But [from my understanding] clusters can't run one system
> image unless they share memory to get at that code.  (or
> are we talking about two different things now?  Sometimes
> it's a PITA to be an old fart.  My apologies if I'm outre'.)

For VMS, all that is needed to run "single system image" is for each node to
somehow have access to the same system disk.  This can be done with fault
tolerance even when most of the nodes don't have direct access to the system
disk.

The way this is accomplished is by having two or more nodes with direct
access to the storage.  This direct access can be provided using CI, DSSI,
SCSI (with suitable adapters and disks), or Fibre Channel.  These several
nodes then serve the storage via the lan to (system) diskless systems which
boot over the network and access the disks using a protocol stack that
provides logical block access to the disks.  All the nodes in the cluster,
including the diskless nodes, run their own filesystem code which is
coordinated via the distributed lock manager.

Also, its important to understand that once the initial system image, and
there are many images required, is loaded, a node booting over the LAN does
exactly the same thing as a node with direct access to the disk.





