If your requirements are different, your solutions are different.

The NASA Mars Rover project, for instance, did not use that methodology
from the manned spacecraft program.  If you remember periodic pauses
in the transmissions from Mars, those have been attributed to the CPU
on the Rover rebooting unexpectedly in response to a fault.  The crucial
thing seems to be that it kept on with its work after a reboot, so the
cost-saving, time-saving approach used for the Mars Rover was acceptable.
I would not like to be a human on a flight programmed in this fashion,
but that is when they use the expensive approach outlined in the article.

