>In article <9id1d7$8mg$1@sunnews.cern.ch>, Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>> In <OVMlOyTpD6CO@eisner.encompasserve.org> Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
>> 
>>>Ideally, tests should be written by someone other than the author
>>>of the code, since the author tends to think in terms of cases
>>>that were anticipated in the writing of the code, whereas it is
>>>also important to test with cases that were _not_ anticipated in
>>>the writing of the code (what if the data file is empty, etc.).
>> 
>> OTOH, only the author knows how to exercise *each* possible execution
>> path in the software module that is to be tested.
>> 
>> So, ideally, tests should be written, independently, by an "insider" and
>> by an "outsider".
>
>While I agree with your conclusion, if the non-author of the code
>cannot figure out what all the execution paths are, the code (and
>comments may not be sufficiently clear).
>
>The person who is not the author of the code, of course, should be
>just as skilled as the person who is the author.

To avoid any kind of biasing, the "outsider" should treat the software
module as a black box, i.e. he should have access only to its 
specification, not to its source code.  Otherwise, he might fall into the
same traps as its implementor.  This was an implicit assumption in my
previous post, so the clarity of the code and the skills of the tester
are irrelevant to the issue.

Dan
