What *I'd* rather get as a customer is a heads-up notification (today) of
possible future changes rather than notification of a fait accompli (whether
today or a year from now):

"Warning:  we see a possible change in the future we previously foresaw.
We're going to go forward with our previous plans for another year (after
all, that's what we've promised you over and over again, and we don't take
such promises lightly), but just in case we're also going to initiate
porting projects to IA64 for Tru64 and VMS.

"This is a win/win proposition.  *If* EV7 doesn't maintain a noticeable
performance lead over McKinley and *if* Intel/HP have as credible an
enhancement roadmap for the next major step after McKinley as we have for
EV8 (so that EV8 may not maintain a significant lead either), *then* we'll
be in a position to wind down Alpha development (but will ensure that EV7xs
are available for many years to come) and turn the porting effort into a
full-blown transition effort (so please let us know over the next year what
support you'd need for such a transition).

"If, on the other hand, Alpha maintains the lead we've always believed it
would, we'll be in a position to pursue the mid-range-to-high-end market
vigorously with proven, leading 64-bit hardware/software platforms, while at
the same time extending our Tru64 and VMS systems' reach into the IA64
market as well.

"Our NSK platform is more special-purpose in nature.  Since it needs to
migrate somewhere anyway, and since its market is more concerned with
absolute reliability than absolute performance, it will be ported to IA64 so
that should the fears some have voiced about Alpha's continued performance
leadership come to pass NSK will not be affected."

At least that's what a company with any reasonable degree of integrity would
have done.  And I don't believe for a minute that 'It would have been easier
for CPQ to defer the announcement of the EV7 end-game for another year':
they much more likely wanted the immediate cash influx from Intel and the
immediate cash savings from sloughing off Alpha and compiler development
costs ASAP.

