Eric Smith wrote:
> Gracefully removing a working CPU isn't that hard.  You force it to
> suspend its running task (or wait for it to be suspended), then don't
> allow it to dispatch a new task.
> 
> Gracefully removing a CPU that failed while executing in user space is
> reasonably easy.  You kill the user task that was running.
> 
> Gracefully recovering from a CPU failure in supervisor space is hard.
> What do you do if that CPU was holding a semaphore at the time?  On some
> systems it isn't even easy to tell what semaphores are being held by
> a given CPU.  And even if you can tell, simply releasing the semaphores
> isn't necessarily an appropriate action.
> 
> But the hardest problem of all is *detecting* a failed CPU.  Some failures
> might be caught by hardware, e.g., parity or ECC on cache, TLB, or
> microcode.  Gross failures (CPU stops executing instructions) might be
> detectable by having another CPU notice that the failed one is no longer
> punching a watchdog.  But many failures are very hard to detect, except
> indirectly when they crash a user program, or the system.  For instance,
> suppose one bit of an adder in the address generation path fails, such that
> at rare intervals the wrong operand is used.

I agree with Eric Smith's analysis but would like to extend it:
the distinction between user/system mode is somewhat misleading for
modern systems, because today user processes may use semaphores (i.e., mutexes)
to protect inter-process critical sections/shared memory, and if
a process is aborted while holding a mutex, rollback is not possible
in general.  In fact, from a whole-system viewpoint (holistic?),
process abortion is always problematic owing to possibly unfinished
modification to data files, etc.  In general, process abort will
require operator intervention to restore the system to a consistent
state.

Transactional systems resolve these problems at the user level.

Furthermore, I would like to know if the failure analysis
for TOPS-20 assumes "fail-silent" nodes, i.e. CPUs that simply
stop working instead of, e.g., randomly corrupting memory.


