> 
> Sure[1].  What else would your Ada divide exception do?  It's
> obviously a sign that something is very sereously broken.  Your
> engine controller can probably reboot ten times a second without
> you noticing anyway.

Along with Ada, unless you are extremely unlucky, comes a culture
of software engineering.  If Jack's automotive shop is as safety
conscious as I hope they are, a culture of software engineering
is something to be embraced, not abhored.  Formal inspection will
certainly check divides to ensure this cannot happen.  One method
is to perform the divide with the datatype Positive, which cannot
be zero.  Making that change and no other will lead to compilation
failure at all other places in the program where a possibly-zero
number is copied into the divisor-to-be.  The only way to coerce
a possibly-zero number into a Positive number is to use something
called Unchecked_Conversion.  They called it Unchecked_Conversion
because they knew there would be objections to the typing involved
for Red_Flag_Formal_Inspectors_Look_Here_For_The_Big_Bugs, but it
serves the same purpose when the time comes for formal inspection.

	http://www.fastcompany.com/online/06/writestuff.html


