In article <3B49AC71.314FE47F@mikron.de>, Bernd Paysan <bpaysan@mikron.de> writes:
> Larry Kilgallen wrote:
>> Along with Ada, unless you are extremely unlucky, comes a culture
>> of software engineering.
> 
> The Ada culture is "Dijkstraist", i.e. more relying on proofs and
> inspection. The second culture is "Knuthian", which means also doing
> tests ("Beware of this program: I prooved it correct, but didn't test
> it"). The third culture is that of Forth programmers, it's twofold.
> First, it means to simplify as much as possible, because simpler
> programs have fewer bugs. Then, it is to test - interactively, unit
> test, whatever you want; a good strategy for highly reliable code is to
> put the unit tests right next to the source, so that compiling will
> always result in running the unit test. This means that when you do the
> testing part of the Knuthian culture right, you don't need much of the
> proof part anymore.
> 
> Baseline: The Forth way and the Ada way of doing things have very little
> in common. Though trusting entirely on inspection and "the compiler will
> tell us all the other bugs" won't lead to reliable software, IMHO. The
> worst culture is the mainstream culture in between, which neither does
> inspection and proofs, nor strict testing.

Certainly testing is required for any software development.
Few organizations are willing to invest the amount of time
required for good testing (boundary and exceptional conditions,
in particular).  Brute force testing is _much_ more expensive
than testing carefully designed for each module.

Ideally, tests should be written by someone other than the author
of the code, since the author tends to think in terms of cases
that were anticipated in the writing of the code, whereas it is
also important to test with cases that were _not_ anticipated in
the writing of the code (what if the data file is empty, etc.).


