>>I think the answer to these is not making cache be write-through but
>>to make sure that the cache doesn't become an overly being liability,
>>which can be achieved with some intelligent writeback scheme (i'd rather
>>not call it scrubing) that would also have much lower impact on memory
>>bandwidth requirements. 

> Wait a minute...There's a difference between write-back and
> write-through.  A write-back means that the cache memeory
> doesn't change if it gets touched.  The write-through means
> that what gets changed in the cache is guaranteed to get
> changed in the memory _at the hardware level_ and doesn't
> require smart programs to ensure data integrity.

Yes and no - write-back cache does not immediately write back data,
in fact it doesn't/shouldn't write it back unless there is a need, but
all write-back caches still have the hardware that ensures the results do
go out of teh cache if needed (coherence). 

Now, in case of a writeback cache it is definately possible that most
cache lines (possibly the whole several MB of them) contain modified
data, including from several applications. That processor going away
would be really bad news, so you'd want to add some smart hardware that
minimised the amount of modified data held in cache if you wanted to
be able to replace the CPU...

