I didn't say that the CURRENT delivery plans were for wildfire, or EV7 for
that matter.  But the initial bids were won before the ES45 existed.

The amount of traffic between nodes for some of the applications is huge.
Wildfire can outperform quadrics between qbbs by a large enough factor to
offset the reduced bandwidth due to memory latency.  The problem is that
wildfire's I/O isn't good enough to meet the bisection bandwidth - wildfire
only support 33MHz PCI while ES45 supports multiple 66MHz PCIs and quadrics
can consume a 66MHz PCI bus.

Wildfire needed PCI-X today - the investment for this was all Compaq's
responsibility and Compaq didn't make the investment.  But this is the way
that Compaq operates: define a technology standard and require it, but don't
do anything that requires investing in making it work or taking the risk of
failing.




