"Dave Weatherall" <djweath@attglobal.net> wrote in message
news:DTiotGxQ0bj6-pn2-TM3sCxdFywBd@localhost...
> On Sun, 8 Jul 2001 05:45:24, Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> wrote:
>
> >
>
> So that's why we get problems (Integer Overflow exceptions IIRC) on
> VAX going trying to write values greater than 127 from wored to byte
> but not an Alpha...

If the behavior is different from VAX to Alpha, it sounds like a bug.  Alpha
has signalling conversion ops to cover the same ones that existed on VAX.
The difference is that on VAX the signaling was a process state while on
Alpha the signalling is encoded in each instruction.  I believe that the
default might be different on the Alpha compiler since most people don't
want the behavior and it does cost in performance when overflow is ignored
dynamically, ie. there is no CPU/ALU state bit that indicates that overflow
should/should not be signaled; its handled in software.




