In a word, no (at least IMNSHO).  Compaq (like DEC in the few years prior to
the acquisition) has shown absolutely zero interest in expanding the VMS
market since acquiring DEC.  They had a plan in place around the time
Capellas took over to kill VMS off, but VMS won at least a temporary
reprieve and actually gained modestly (single-digit annual percentage
growth) after that (until two weeks ago) despite still-negligible marketing.

VMS is unlikely to survive the loss of Alpha (a significant percentage of
VMS customers wouldn't trust Compaq to empty their trash at this point) in
anything like its current state of marginal health, especially as the only
development effort Compaq has offered to offset the blow of losing Alpha is
the port to IA64 - something that AFAICT no one in the VMS community ever
asked for, and certainly not if the cost was losing Alpha (there has been
interest over the years in an *IA32* port to raise low-end volume, but an
IA32 port as an adjunct to Alpha-based VMS is a far cry from an IA64 port as
a substitute for Alpha-based VMS).

At best, VMS may limp along in close-to-maintenance-mode long enough to be
ported to IA64.  But if the current sales (the most recent figures I'm aware
of were $4 billion annual revenue/$800 million annual profit attributable to
VMS, a couple of years ago) were insufficient to spark any Compaq interest
in marketing/developing/expanding the platform, the
likely-significantly-diminished sales over the 3 years the port seems
