I do not understand this statement.  Which part of the technology do
you consider 'neat'? and why does that make it a niche market?

I hope that this isn't a sidewise shot at NUMA in general--which is
not so much 'neat' as it is necessary.  I also claim that in N years,
even most small systems (2-4 nodes) will be ccNUMA--with N as some
finite number < 10.

As for Wildfire's particular implementation of a ccNUMA--it is an
incredibly interesting, though complex, system.  The cache coherence
protocol of Wildfire should be studied in multiprocessor architecture
courses--as it is chalk full of trade-offs and
architecture/implementation decisions (whether all the right decisions
were made is an open question, but I think those guys did a good job).

Compared to EV7, though, I don't think you can find two ccNUMA
protocols that are more different.  Maybe I'll write a paper one of
these days contrasting Wildfire, EV7, and EV8 protocols.  The point is
that the design space in ccNUMA is very large--and Wildfire, while a
solid system, was just the starting point for Alpha.

