>+---------------
>| Bill, with one exception I agree with all you said here...
>....
>| It's possible for I/O to be physically attached just to one
>| physical unit (processor(s) and/or memory) with its own I/O bus,
>| making it available to others via the memory hierarchy. E.g., if
>| the bus is memory-mapped, all the busses are in the memory space
>| of all the processors, no matter which unit the bus is in. 
>| 
>| It's still the case then that every processor has the same
>| "logical" capabilities; just one of them does it a tad faster
>| than others.
>+---------------
>
>And this is exactly the situation with the SGI Origin 2000/3000 family.
>"Nodes" (node cards in O2k; "C-Bricks" in O3k) are the unit of CPU &
>memory modularity (2 CPUs/4GB in O2k; 4 CPUs/8GB in O3k), interconnected
>via the ccNUMA "NUMAlink". I/O busses (of which a single system can have
>hundreds!) are each connected through a small crossbar via "XIO" to (up 
to)
>two nodes, one of which is assigned at boot time to be the "master" node
>for that particular bus. In the case of PCI, up to six PCI busses share
>the XIO crossbar to a pair of nodes, and (usually) each node is the 
"master"
>for half of the busses.
>
>PIOs from any CPU in the system to a device on some bus must travel across
>the NUMAlink to the directly-attached "master" node for that bus. 
Likewise,
>DMA from a device on that bus must flow through its assigned master 
node[*]
>into node-local memory or across the NUMAlink to any other memory in the
>system. Though the access times vary depending on relative locations, all
>CPUs can PIO to all devices on all busses, and all devices can DMA 
directly
>to all memory in the system[**].
>
>Notes:
>[*] The GSN (HIPPI-6400) interface is the sole exception to the "access
>    only through the master node" rule. Through some careful extensions
>    to the usual Origin protocols, GSN (which optionally has two XIO
>    connections into the system) can actually be doing DMA through as
>    many as *four* separate nodes at once (which it needs, given its 
rate).
>
>[**] Because they only provide 32 bits of DMA address, "A32" PCI devices
>    (whether they're D32 or D64) can only access a subset of main memory
>    at any instant. However, the mapping may be (and is) 
>changed dynamically
>    by host software (though obviously there's a 
>performance cost for that).
>    A64-capable PCI devices have no such restriction, and thus are greatly
>    preferred to A32-only (but many commodity PC cards are A32-only!!).
>
>+---------------
>| This is all "SMP" means, anyway. The software may or may not be
>| optimized to deal with the performance asymmetries.
>+---------------
>
>In the case of Irix on Origin, I dare say the software *is* highly tuned
>to deal with the asymmetries!! E.g., NUMA-aware drivers 
>can choose (or not)
>to preferentially allocate memory for descriptor rings, mailboxes, and
>"busy" buffers on the master node for a device. Any CPU can be assigned
>to handle interrupts from any device, but the software tries to assign
>a CPU on the master node for a device, or (if those are overbooked) to
>some CPU on a "nearby" node. [And of course you can manually override
>those assignments with config files, if you know what you're doing --
>useful for massive real-time I/O apps like media streaming.]

