"Paul DeMone" <pdemone@igs.net> wrote in message
news:3B469F39.618E0ADE@igs.net...
> mulp wrote:
> > The reason for not implementing byte/word ops was to avoid having to do
a
> > read/modify/write operation to update memory.  To write a byte into
memory,
> > you must read the longword containing the byte, do any required ECC
> > corrections, insert the byte, compute a new ECC, and then update memory.
>
> Nice try but no. The original Alpha ISA left out 8 and 16 bit loads
> and stores to simplify the load aligner shift network between the
> data cache and the CPU registers/bypass paths, a common critical
> path.

I forgot the argument about the aligner network, but since I don't have much
insight into chip logic design (yet! - I've only been doing computer stuff
for 33 years).

Here's a quote from "Alpha - Implementations and Architecture" by Dileep
Bhandarkar, copyright 1996.

---
3.2.5  Byte Load or Store Instructions

The Alpha architecture has no byte load or store instructions and no
implicit unaligned accesses. There also are no partial-register writes. The
byte load/store instructions and unaligned accesses found in some RISC
architectures can also be a performance bottleneck. They require an extra
byte shifter in the speed-critical load and store paths, and they force a
hard choice in fast cache design.  The partial-register writes found in
other RISC architectures can also be a
bottleneck because they require masking and shifting in the fundamental
operation of accessing a register.

An additional problem with byte stores is that it requires a read-modify
write sequence for write-back cache memories that implement single-bit error
correction code (ECC). This requires hidden sequencer hardware and hidden
state to hold the cache word temporarily. The sequencer tends to slow down
ordinary full-cache-word stores. The need for byte stores tends to ripple
throughout the memory subsystem design, making each piece a little more
complicated and a little slower. With non-replicated hidden state, it is
difficult to issue another byte store until the first one finishes.

Instead of including byte load/store, we followed the RISC philosophy of
exposing hidden computation as a sequence of many simple, fastinstructions.
In the Alpha architecture, a byte load is done as an explicit load/shift
sequence a byte store as an explicit load/modify/store sequence.

Despite the rationale listed above, the lack of byte load/stores remains one
of the most controversial omissions in the Alpha architecture. Old byte
intensive code runs rather slow unless it is replaced by new code that does
64-bit operations wherever possible.
---




