> Greg Pfister wrote:
> >
> > Bill, with one exception I agree with all you said here,
> > particularly with regard to fault tolerance / high avaialability.
> > SMPs are definitely not architecturally fault-tolerant, and they
> > *will* die completely if only one processor bites the dust. Ditto
> > NUMA systems.
>
> I got some complaints about the above offline from parts of the
> audience who are familiar with IBM zSeries, S/390 and perhaps
> S/370 and earlier models (I don't know when the feature was first
> introduced). Mainframes, y'know?
>
> Those systems *can* lose a processor and keep on truckin',
> possibly even continuing the process that was running on the
> failed CPU at the time (if the error was of the right kind). This
> is done with something called the PAF (Processor Availablility
> Feature), which supports moving the state of the process out of a
> failed unit onto a working one, and re-starting it there --
> usually heading it right into cleanup/kill it code, but sometimes
> not.
>
> Outside of the duplexed / TMR / other hardware-fault-tolerant
> arena, no other "SMP" on the planet has this, and indeed one
> shouldn't talk about these systems as "SMPs." They're properly
> "TCMPs", Tightly-Coupled MultiProcessors, which I personally
> consider a variant on SMPs because they have the above as well as
> other distinguishing characteristics (cache coherence rules
> follow "observable order" definitions, for example).
>
> It is architecturally visible, since supervisory code has to deal
> with this. But I personally wouldn't consider this as part of the
> usual definition of SMP architecture. It is part of the TCMP
> architecture.



I'm a little lost with all of the terminology going on here, but I worked on
an 1108 multiporcessor system in the early 1970s that could have a procssor
fail and keep running, at least theoretically. However, many times the
failure was one that screwed up something else like dribbling over memory
that caused the sysem to fail.  Note that this was before the S360 had any
tightly coupled multiprocessors.  Later systems in the 1100/2200 series had
duplexed ALUs etc. such that a failing processor could be detected early and
taken out of the configuration transparently, much as the current IBM
mainframes do.

BTW, memory could fail and if it contained only user tasks, they would be
aborted and the system kept running.  A bad memory that contained Exec
code/data was fatal to the whole system.  Well, actually, it failed the
program that had code in the failed memory, only that program happened to be
the operating system  :-(.


