Sure... anyone doing lights out departmental internet processing can't go look 
at the machine to see what failed...

I've got 50 servers an hour away running Linux.  They've got to have a 
fail over path that just works, since contractual obligations with 
my customers don't allow downtime of more than minutes.

Since they don't need to be SMP (at this time -- although many are) 
I can just remotely shoot any malfunctioning (or suspected bad) boxes
remotely via TCP/IP enabled power strips.

The load balancing devices in line would round-robin new requests
to the next machine up.  (There are multiples of each machine
on line...). Users already on line would lose their session and have to 
re-login.  (This is NOT an E-Commerce app...)

I also can get the console from any of them over the internet or modem.
(Either graphics or serial console...)

But both these situations are less good than true VMS style clustering
would be.

An equivalent of an HSC link would allow the disks to be shared
to all cpu's and multiple systems could run one system image.

Upgrades could be done while the cluster was up -- by switching systems
image one machine at a time.

Systems could be Managed rather than just Administered (to).

But that's the difference between the VAX/VMS I did back in 4.2 days
and the Unix stuff I've done since.  Most of the Unix stuff has been 
brute forced -- since the hardware and software weren't designed together
to do what the VAX/VMS mix did...

Far to often capabilities are bolted on...  I worked with two vendor's
Unix clusters (one in the early 90's that Sun took a lot of ideas from)
and they were so complex to manage I haven't gone near them in the last 
8 years.

Bill

