"Rich Walker" <rw@shadow.org.uk> wrote in message
news:m366d8vz7u.fsf@lin-pc.shadow.local...

...

> Interesting in the following sense:
> single filesystem served from single fileserver machine with a
> logical volume manager mounting network block devices from other
> machines with lots of disks
>
> is I believe do-able today with stock linux and NFS.

Not when you throw in the reasonably-fast fail-over requirement:  that
requires a 'careful-update' file system such as VMS's or a log-protected
file system such as Reiserfs or ext3fs (neither of which AFAIK is quite
ready for prime-time) or a log-structured file system (which AFAIK doesn't
yet exist at all on Linux) to avoid fsck restart latency.

I think IBM just released JFS in something that may no longer be a beta-test
version, so that might qualify.  But there still may not yet be any
underlying/surrounding fail-over support available on Linux (though multiple
groups are working in this area), including a standard mechanism for IP
address fail-over (unless NFS now has some internal fail-over support that
avoids the need for this).

>
> single filesystem served from multiple machines with lots of
> disks without fileserver+LVM+NBD
>
> would be well worth reading the papers on how it worked.
>
> It might be that something approaching the latter could be created from
> a collection of machines serving disks to a collection of fileservers
> that collaborated to ensure their clients *saw* a coherent filesystem
> without having to create a coherent filesystem.
>
> So, rather than storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
>    with replication
>
> we have
> storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
> ^
> |
> V
> storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
> ^
> |
> V
> storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
>
> with redundancy built in such that, after a write is committed, loss of
> any one piece of hardware is not a problem.

The above diagram - with all the 'fileserver' entries removed - is what was
being recently discussed in comp.arch/comp.arch.storage in the 'commodity
storage servers' thread (the storage boxes cooperate to serve files or space
on logical volumes directly to clients).

>
> I guess this requires the fileserver<->fileserver bandwidth to
> approximate the aggregate write-bandwidth of the clients, whilst
> permitting the aggregate read-bandwith of the clients to saturate all
> available links.

Precisely.

>
> But the case I'm interested in does not differentiate between
> fileservers and clients. All boxes in the group provide storage to the
> common storage pool.

There's nothing inherent in your diagram above (with the 'fileserver'
entries removed) that would prevent the client/storage box interface from
being an internal one in a single box supporting both - which also halves
the total number of links required.  But the clients have to trust each
other a lot more than when they're separated from the storage servers and
can rely upon storage-server protection mechanisms.  Clients that cooperate
to export a single, stable application to a much wider external world (e.g.,
Web servers) tend to fall into this category, but clients that may be
running virtually anything (which can compromise their robustness) or are in
the service of individuals (e.g., workstations) who may elect to reboot them
at any time may better be kept separate from the storage servers.

- bill





