> >units.  The pairs operate in lockstep, but don't have to be compared.
> >(There's still comparision within the FRC pairs).  Intel called this QMR
> >(Quad Modular Redundancy).  If one FRC pair detects a failure, the other
> >FRC pair still has valid state.  The system can take the failed pair
> >offline, and possibly run diagnostics to determine which unit in the FRC
> >pair is actually bad.  The system can either run the remaining FRC pair
> >in a non-fault-tolerant manner, shut it down and move its load to
> >remaining fault-tolerant nodes, or join the remaining FRC pair with a
> spare
> >to bring it back to QMR.
>
> It used to be that this kind of care was necessary only in
> places where men couldn't go to look at the machine...like space.
> Are there now other niches where this kind of care is required?

Anywhere you don't want to have to tolerate the chance of non-'fail-stop'
(some people say 'fail-fast') failure-mode operation - i.e., anywhere you
want to ensure that incorrect results won't be generated (rather than assume
that any failure will stop the node *before* any incorrect result has been
generated).

Keeping a system running is something clusters do very well (though the
glitch they experience when a single node fails is usually something on the
order of a second rather than a millisecond).  But ensuring *correct*
operation is something they do less well, since they depend on the hardware
either running correctly or not running at all.

Tandem's entire market is based on this distinction.  So was Status's (I
guess they still exist, but I think IBM owns them now - not that Tandem's
situation is much different...).  Enterprises like bankers and stock
exchanges appreciate it.

- bill

