Alpha has always had a great following in comp.arch; but what still
holds true is that they never build a proper high-end system to support
is; they let the aenemic "Turbo Laser" systems linger way too many
CPU generations and then came out with the "GS320" which drew
"now that's big iron" headlines in comp.arch.

But let's face it, bringing out a 32CPU machine in 2000 with a bisection
bandwidth of 12GB/s (like the '96 Starfire) and a local/remote latency
of 320/960ns, the latter being more than twice the 100MHz E10k memory
latency; requiuring > 80% of memory refences to be local for like performance).
Such systems are useful only for limited applications and not
those that are the most interesting ones, commercially.

Having read both the admiration for the GS320 as well as the initial
reports of "Wildfire" non-disclosure meetings that had people awe-struck,
I can only conclude that Alpha followers miss even the most basic
understanding of what makes systems successful in commercial applications.
It really isn't all about marketing.

Oh, "EV7 will fix all that"; bit late, if you ask me.

Casper
