> 
> I think you are.  But I've got to say that Sander's apples sound a lot more
> like what my understanding of SMP is than your kumquats.  E.g., considering
> a single-CPU system to be a degenerate case of SMP does not strike me as
> particularly strange, though most people wouldn't think of it that way save
> in a conversation like the current one.
> 
> > >
> > >If you have a 5 cpu system I see no reason why any of the cpus shouldn't
> > >be able to access the disk system as long as it has at least one port
> > >and you didn't screw something up.
> >
> > The data just produced on one CPU has to follow wire to
> > get stored on a disk that is physically connected to another
> > CPU.  It ain't done with magic.
> 
> But it ain't done that way on most modern SMP systems, either:  data
> produced on all CPUs gets placed on some sort of bus that the disk is
> connected to - there's no such thing as a CPU private to one of them.  I
> suspect that's why Sander suggested you were thinking of something more like
> clustering (or asymmetric MP where, e.g., the system runs on one processor
> that controls all the disks and user processes run on others).
> 
> >
> > > ...If hardware being symmetric is not
> > >clear... well, then you are way out of date or again, use some really
> > >strange definition.
> >
> > Symmetric means that the computer system does not have a
> > configuration where a point failure disables total delivery
> > of computer service.
> 
> My understanding is that modern SMP systems not only do not guarantee the
> lack of any single point of failure (that's something close to a
> triple-negative, I think...) but in most cases don't tolerate the failure of
> any one of their CPUs:  modern SMP is mostly about aggregating the
> performance of multiple CPUs, not about availability.
> 
> Corrections to the above would be most welcome:  I wouldn't want to cling to
> a major misunderstanding in this area any more than I suspect you would.
> 
> - bill


