The performance gains for EV8 were for one risky step, SMT, and for more
pipes giving diminishing returns, and for higher clock rate by faster gates
and more cache.

From a design standpoint, only one significant change was being made in EV8,
SMT.  And this was being done in a conservative fashion.  4 virtual CPUs
were considered along with making the CPUs visible to the OS and/or
application, but instead a more conservative approach was taken to reduce
risk.   The other changes are relatively straight forward: make use of the
improved technology to throw more gates at the problem, and find the
bottlenecks and remove them.  A major bottleneck is access to memory.

(I suppose a packaging engineer might argue that the doubling of bandwidth
and power by doubling the number of pins, etc. isn't a straight forward
change....)

