>> There's a saying in the aviation industry that with a big enough engine you
>> can fly a barn door. Well, the x86 architecture is a barn door, and it's
>> had the biggest engine in the business strapped to it... of course it flies,
>> but that just means it's got an impressive engine, not that the architecture
>> is a good one.
>> 
>> How many engines like that can intel run simultaneously? Is IA64 a barn door
>> or a gossamer albatross?
>
>IA64 is the whole damn barn including the foundation. Remember, IA64
>incorporates x86 as an architectural component. Just like a barn
>encompasses the barn door.

I don't believe that Intel have ever taken quite that position, and
certainly HP haven't.  Intel has vacillated over whether the IA-64
architecture should include any IA-32 compatibility and, if so, how
much; but they have generally said that it doesn't.  Whether any of
the implementations of the IA-64 should include IA-32 support is
something on which they have taken virtually every possible position,
including some that are physically impossible.

But I believe that the general view is that the IA-64 architecture
is separate, though with a few compatibility features, but that the
initial implementations will include a great many extensions for
extra compatibility.


