I am not saying your description of what happened at DEC is incorrect.  I
wasn't there and don't know.  But your generalities about MBAs are clearly
wrong.  An MBA would see the set of products/services that a company has as
equivalent to a portfolio of stocks.  You want to have more than one
project, just like you wouldn't invest all of your money in only one stock -
in order to minimize diversifyable risk.  You ajust your investments in
different projects (different stocks) based on your understanding and
beliefs about the future cash flow (similar to profit) potential of each of
them.  Sometimes you sell a stock you used to like because you believe that
its future growth opportunity is less than some other stock which you use
the proceeds to buy.  Now no one gets this right all the time - plenty of
mistakes are made.  I bet that you have invested in some stocks that didn't
go up as much as you thought they would.

This is all just a little basic Theory of Finance.

