Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> writes:

> It seems natural to subset the x86 component of IA64 for software
> emulation like the uVAX-II did for the CISC-ier VAX instructions.
> But so far Intel has not set any time table to do so.

IIRC x86 has done this for many years. Just the software emulation is 
implemented in microcode, not in some user loadable program. All the 
optimization guides for Athlon or Pentium have long lists of what 
instructions are real or what instructions are "emulated" and they tell
you to avoid the later ones as far as possible :-)

For me it seems the main drawbacks of modern X86 with SSE are the
requirement to detect self modifying code, missing address space IDs 
(requiring TLB flushes for every process context switch), too many
locked (=slow) memory accesses designed into critical operations (like 
interrupts or privilege switch or any pte access), too small (or too big; 
2/4MB is too big) page sizes, too rigid memory ordering model
