What I like is his explanation how marketing works. I think he stopped a
bit early, though. It's easy to explain that engineer-driven companies
have bad marketing, because the enigneers don't see the need for it -
and that companies with worse products try to compensate with better
marketing. However, when such a company gets in the hand of a
marketing-driven company (and one can't say Compaq isn't such a
company), why doesn't it work there? Jealous divisions are one
explanation, but it looks like what marketing people realy hate are good
engineers. They rather market a worse product (to show how good they
are) instead of supporting the better mousetrap.

Or it is something else, e.g. price. Technical superior products result
in sales milking the existent customer base - that also somehow happend
with Alpha. After all, there is nothing technological that stops selling
Alphas at the same price an Athlon goes (except that the .18u shrink
EV68 still isn't ready); you could even use the same boards, just
replace the BIOS (given that EV68 would ship in the same package and is
within the voltage/current limits of an Athlon board) - if this ramps up
the number of units sold, taking a loss lead is always a good idea.

The other battlefield is software. Apparently, Alpha was never marketed
as workstation (e.g. EDA tools or such like). This means that noone
pushed Cadence or Synopsys (etc.) into supporting Alpha, you have only
choice between PA-RISC and SPARC (and for some tools x86/Linux).

