Bill Todd wrote:

> 
> Had Alpha been considered of any strategic importance to Compaq two years
> ago, the AlphaNT base would have been encouraged rather than left to vanish,
> and the credibility of AlphaNT (and Win64 on Alpha:  do you really believe
> that Microsoft would have extended its field test for more than two years
> just to wait for IA64 to hit the stores?) would have bolstered industry
> interest in the platform to at least some degree.



I'm not sure I understand the point you are making here, are you saying
Win64 was ready to ship along with NT5/win2000?  That doesn't really
match the history of the product as I remember it... While it is true that
Alpha builds of NT5 up to and including 2128 were released during the beta,
(and it was very solid at that time, the final build being 2195), and
I'm sure the kernel group continued doing Win64 work on Alpha (on
DS20s, I think?) post-release, Win64 was not a deliverable within the 
NT5 project...

And once NT5/Win2000 shipped, Win64 would probably have ended up in 
Whistler anyway unless they had released a "Windows 2000 64-bit edition"
just to support Alpha some months later... One reason to make such a
release would be for TPC bragging purposes (SQL/64 on Win64 on a
shared-nothing set of ES40s, whatever).. Doesn't Jim Gray's Terraserver
"prood of concept" system run on AlphaNT? It used to, anyway...


I haven't seen whistler/NT 5.1 on IA64 hardware yet, so I can't
comment on its "readiness"... Had they continued to use Alpha as the 
base Win64 platform, they would probably have saved dev time that much 
is certain...


