The source of this disagreement is Terry's (I suspect unintentional)
obfuscation above of the difference between engineers practicing engineering
(what he calls 'Engineering' above) and engineers trying to be managers (the
people he correctly describes as driving DEC decisions around a decade ago).
I inadvertently bought into this confusion in one of my responses:  it's
easy to miss.

Decisions at DEC started being driven by engineers trying to be managers,
rather than by the practicing engineers, some time in the early '80s.  And
one of the things that those engineers did in attempting to be effective
managers was try to learn from MBA types who knew little-to-nothing about
DEC's business (the conventional MBA wisdom at the time being that
understanding the basics of a business was an unnecessary distraction to
managing it well).

