In comp.arch Anne & Lynn Wheeler <lynn@garlic.com> wrote:
> name99@mac.com (Maynard Handley) writes:
>> While I was aware that the higher end machines were multiple chips, I
>> thought those low-end desktops were single chip, but heck, I can believe
>> it if you say they were also multichip.

> Announced early 1990

> 320 ... desktop

Oddly enough, I have a 320H (I think it is) here that's been
functioning as a web server for many years and which had the fan in
the power supply give up the ghost last week.

As a result I had occasion to take it apart, and noted that the 
processor board has something like nine large heat-sinked chips
plus a big PROM labeled Microcode rev <something>.  So it doesn't
look very "single chip" to me :-)

The machine is long since off of support, and IBM chose to weld the
power supply case together into a single unmaintainable blob.  Add
the fact that the fan seems to have rotational sensing support that
causes the power supply to scram if the fan doesn't turn for about
three seconds and this machine is heading for the scrap heap.

The inside case molding has a manufacture date of (IIRC) July 1990.

G.

P.S. We actually have it running at the moment by pointing a large
houshold fan at the dead PS fan.  The airflow is enough to drive the
bad fan (backwards :-) and keep the PS from tripping off.


