As do I. Let's review: when Compaq announced its intention to purchase
Digital, the AlphaNT run rate was in fact >15 percent. Six months later (due
to benign or malign neglect on the part of the Wintel box-pushers, and the
lack of statement of strong support from CPQ) the run rate was below 2
percent. The entire AlphaNT effort (including tools being ported from VMS
and NSK to Microsoft-land) was about $150M per year). Obviously this could
not be sustained by the evaporating shipment numbers. (Recall that at the
time, Intel speed increases had offset much of the advantage enjoyed by
32-bit AlphaNT, and recall also that His Gateness demanded that Compaq pay
the ongoing freight for the AlphaNT64 port--which Compaq already had
developed, and which His Gateness refused to release since it might "offer
an unfair advantage to one architecture over another.")

Ancient history, though. One can debate the rationale behind the decision ad
infinatum, but the fact remains that Compaq's Alpha roadmap ends with EV7x.
Whether VMS and Tru64 and NSK suffer a similar fate in a similar timeframe
remains to be seen.








