But it's just common sense.  Take two architects, one hardware
and the other software.  To be architects, they have a few
things in common.  1.  They don't suffer fools at all.  2.
Most of the time they know better than anybody else. 

In order for each to be able to critique the other's ideas,
they really, really, really do have know that the other guy/gal
isn't an idiot.  It is not efficient to determine this via
a net link.  In some cases, having a 5 foot divider between
them will work.  In other cases, having a foot thick floor
to ceiling wall has to be in place.  It all depends on the
ego mix.  

If these two architects are working together from the beginning,
the software architect says, "Gee, I could really use a twiddle
bit in the hardware.  It would make my life so much easier."
The hardware engineer determines that at twiddle is just a flick
of his wrist and puts it in creating an innovation.

In another case, the hardware architect who has been trying to
incorporate a piece of spec but keeps running in to walls
says to the software guy, "Gee, I just can't get this to work
out."  And the software guy says, "Forget about it.  I can do
that with my software.  Just give me this info and I'll take
of it for you."

And, boys and girls, that is how a computer _system_ should
be designed.

/BAH

