Digital, in the mid- 80's had no aversion whatsoever to "obsoleting"
their own systems.  And I can only assume that it WAS marketing, not
engineering who made these decisions.  One model would come out, then 5
months later, something far faster and cheaper would arrive killing the
revenue stream of the earlier product line. Intel made BILLIONS from
knowing how to milk the revenue stream from the 286, 386, and 486
architectures because THEIR marketing people didn't pop quaaludes for
breakfast, and probably graduated high school.  I suspect the only
reason the pentium was released so quickly (too quickly) was that Intel
sensed competition from other processors.  In the absence of
competition, it is prudent NOT TO KILL your own product lines.  And with
proprietary hardware in the mid 80's, as was the vogue for IBM and the
like, there often was no direct competition because they has their
customers locked up. For customers, switching to another computer vendor
back then was simply too costly.  As a company, when you've done your
R+D, and manufactured a product, you want as much sales from that
product as possible, because there are no more initial costs so you just
get to keep selling the thing.  Ever heard of an operating system called
DOS by a company called Microsoft?  Yes Virginia, there was in fact a
version 6 of this product.  For at LEAST 7 years, DOS remained virtually
unchanged, while gates just pocketed the cash.  If it weren't for the
likes of Xerox PARC and Apple, old Billy would be serving up DOS version
45.1 as we speak!  You want to be successful? Don't kill your own
revenue streams from products unless incipient competition makes it
imperative.  Anyone want to buy the brooklyn bridge? I'm selling it
cheap, and I've only got three left.  :)


HM

