Yes, *if* you go back a decade.  More recently, do you have any evidence to
offer that fiascos such as the DEC-initiated radical shift in focus from
Alpha systems to PC platforms (and attempts to migrate VMS customers to NT)
and the repeated slashings of the work force were engineering-driven?

If the decision to drop Alpha in fact came out of engineering (instead of
being a report in the same vein as the one that described the 'unbelievable
enthusiasm' customers had for dropping Alpha), it was quite likely because
engineering were given the premise that Compaq would in any case continue
pushing Intel hardware at any and every opportunity and squeezing Alpha
funding accordingly, rather than support the architecture and the systems on
it in the manner they merited.

So far, the available evidence suggests that Alpha was axed (after 3 years
of tepid support by Compaq in everything save its assurances to customers of
its long-term commitment) because Compaq has no interest in supporting its
own technology but only in assembling and marketing technology developed by
others.  VMS alone, without marketing support, brought in sufficient revenue
to support continued Alpha development:  if Compaq had had any interest in
actually trying to *compete* with Alpha rather than simply rake in residual
revenue from an unwanted artifact of the DEC acquisition, it could easily
have done so successfully.

